LS_REG('part',8,{"toc":[[2,"h2","4.1 Three tiers of government and Schedules 5 to 9 (तीन तहको सरकार र अनुसूची ५ देखि ९)"],[2,"h13","4.2 Provincial executive, Provincial Assembly and provincial institutions (प्रदेश कार्यपालिका, प्रदेश सभा र प्रदेशका संस्था)"],[2,"h24","4.3 Local government: structure, powers and the Local Government Operation Act (स्थानीय सरकार: संरचना, अधिकार र स्थानीय सरकार सञ्चालन ऐन, २०७४)"],[2,"h35","4.4 Inter-governmental relations and fiscal federalism (अन्तर-सरकारी सम्बन्ध र वित्तीय संघीयता)"],[2,"h46","4.5 Elections and the political system (निर्वाचन र राजनीतिक प्रणाली)"],[2,"h57","4.6 Civil service and the Public Service Commissions (निजामती सेवा र लोक सेवा आयोग)"],[2,"h68","4.7 Good governance, right to information and control of corruption (सुशासन, सूचनाको हक र भ्रष्टाचार नियन्त्रण)"],[2,"h79","4.8 Electronic governance and public service delivery (विद्युतीय सुशासन र सार्वजनिक सेवा प्रवाह)"],[2,"h90","Chapter 4 — End matter"],[3,"h91","A. Quick Revision"],[3,"h92","B. Top 20 facts"],[3,"h93","C. Top 25 MCQs"],[3,"h94","D. Five short-answer questions"],[3,"h95","E. Common mistakes"],[3,"h96","F. Status of the facts in this chapter"],[3,"h97","G. References"]],"html":"<h1 id=\"h1\">PART 8 — CHAPTER 4: Federalism and Governance (संघीयता र शासन प्रणाली)<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Book-plan section<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>D<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Priority<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>Moderate<\/strong>; <strong>High<\/strong> in Bagmati and Karnali<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Official units<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Bagmati: unit 1.8 &quot;Governance system — constitution, political system, three tiers of government, districts&quot; — <strong>3 questions<\/strong> (as read). Karnali: periodic plans, budget and the <strong>revenue jurisdiction of the three tiers<\/strong> sit inside the economy unit, and electronic service delivery and <strong>e-governance<\/strong> sit inside the science and technology unit (the unit numbers could not be read, so none is stated here). In the other five provinces no General Knowledge unit of Paper I is set aside for governance. There it enters through current affairs, and it is core material for the later subjective papers.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Province-specific wording<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Bagmati names the &quot;three tiers of government&quot; and &quot;districts&quot;. Karnali names revenue jurisdiction and e-governance.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Facts checked on<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>11 November 2026 (25 Kartik 2083 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Questions in this chapter<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>65 MCQs (40 in topics + 25 at the end) and 13 short-answer questions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>How to read the marks<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>🔄 <strong>UPDATE<\/strong> — a recent fact that may change. Check it again before your examination.<\/li>\n<li>⚖️ <strong>DISPUTED<\/strong> — published sources disagree. Learn both versions.<\/li>\n<li><code>[Pattern-Based – Reconstructed]<\/code> — written to an official question format; not a real past question.<\/li>\n<li><code>[Expected – Practice]<\/code> — an original practice question on a point the syllabus names.<\/li>\n<li>No question in this chapter is a verified past question.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>A note on dates.<\/strong> BS means Bikram Sambat; AD means the Common Era. Every BS–AD pair in this chapter was worked out with a calendar-conversion tool.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A note on the text used.<\/strong> Article numbers and figures from the Constitution were checked against the same two copies used for Chapter 3: an English translation read Article by Article, and a Nepali text used for the key figures. Both came from public collections, <strong>not<\/strong> from the Nepal Law Commission's own website, which could not be opened directly during this research. Where the two copies disagree, this chapter follows the Nepali text and says so. Facts about <strong>Acts<\/strong> (for example the Local Government Operation Act) come from extracts of the Law Commission's pages shown in search results and from secondary sources; the full Acts were not read. Section F at the end of the chapter says which facts were checked and how.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A note on words.<\/strong> The English translation calls a प्रदेश a &quot;State&quot; and a प्रदेश प्रमुख a &quot;Chief of State&quot;. This book says <strong>province<\/strong> and <strong>Province Chief<\/strong>. A गाउँपालिका is a <strong>rural municipality<\/strong>; a स्थानीय तह is a <strong>local level<\/strong>. English names of Acts are translations of the Nepali names.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Federalism at a glance<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level<\/th>\n<th>Number<\/th>\n<th>Executive<\/th>\n<th>Legislature<\/th>\n<th>List of powers<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Federation (संघ)<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>Council of Ministers under the Prime Minister<\/td>\n<td>Federal Parliament: House of Representatives (275) and National Assembly (59)<\/td>\n<td>Schedule 5; shares Schedules 7 and 9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Province (प्रदेश)<\/td>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>Provincial Council of Ministers under the Chief Minister<\/td>\n<td>Provincial Assembly, one house (550 seats in the seven Assemblies)<\/td>\n<td>Schedule 6; shares Schedules 7 and 9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local level (स्थानीय तह)<\/td>\n<td>753<\/td>\n<td>Village Executive or Municipal Executive<\/td>\n<td>Village Assembly or Municipal Assembly<\/td>\n<td>Schedule 8; shares Schedule 9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The courts are <strong>not<\/strong> divided among the levels. One court system — the Supreme Court, 7 High Courts and 77 District Courts — serves the whole country (Chapter 3, topic 3.8). Each local level also has a three-member Judicial Committee, which is not a court.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Topics<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>4.1 Three tiers of government and Schedules 5 to 9 · 4.2 Provincial executive, Provincial Assembly and provincial institutions · 4.3 Local government: structure, powers and the Local Government Operation Act · 4.4 Inter-governmental relations and fiscal federalism · 4.5 Elections and the political system · 4.6 Civil service and the Public Service Commissions · 4.7 Good governance, right to information and control of corruption · 4.8 Electronic governance and public service delivery<\/p>\n<p>Chapter 3 already gives the Parts and Schedules of the Constitution, the federal executive, Parliament, the courts and the constitutional bodies. This chapter refers to them and does not repeat them.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h2\">4.1 Three tiers of government and Schedules 5 to 9 (तीन तहको सरकार र अनुसूची ५ देखि ९)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h3\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>Bagmati's unit 1.8 names the &quot;three tiers of government&quot;. The commonest question asks you to match a Schedule with its list, or a subject with its level. Everything else in this chapter rests on this topic.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h4\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Federalism<\/strong> (संघीयता) means that the constitution itself divides the power of the State among levels of government. Each level has its own elected government, its own budget and its own list of work. No level is simply a branch office of another.<\/p>\n<p>Before 2072 BS Nepal was a <strong>unitary<\/strong> state: one central government held the power and lent some of it to districts, villages and towns. The Constitution of Nepal changed that. <strong>Article 56<\/strong> says the main structure of the State has <strong>three levels<\/strong>: the <strong>Federation<\/strong> (संघ), the <strong>Province<\/strong> (प्रदेश) and the <strong>Local level<\/strong> (स्थानीय तह).<\/p>\n<p>The work of each level is written in five lists, <strong>Schedules 5 to 9<\/strong>. Three lists are <strong>exclusive<\/strong> (एकल अधिकार) — one level alone. Two are <strong>concurrent<\/strong> (साझा अधिकार) — shared. Anything not written in any list belongs to the Federation. The three levels are meant to work together, not compete: their relations rest on <strong>cooperation, coexistence and coordination<\/strong> (सहकारिता, सहअस्तित्व र समन्वय).<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h5\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Part 5<\/strong> of the Constitution (Articles 56 to 60) is &quot;Structure of State and Distribution of State Power&quot;.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 56 (1):<\/strong> three levels — Federation, Province and Local level.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 56 (3):<\/strong> the provinces are made up of the districts listed in <strong>Schedule 4<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 56 (4):<\/strong> the local level consists of <strong>rural municipalities (गाउँपालिका), municipalities (नगरपालिका) and District Assemblies (जिल्ला सभा)<\/strong>. Federal law fixes the number of wards.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 56 (5):<\/strong> federal law may create a <strong>special, protected or autonomous region<\/strong> (विशेष, संरक्षित वा स्वायत्त क्षेत्र) for social and cultural protection or economic development.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 56 (6):<\/strong> all three levels must protect Nepal's freedom, sovereignty, territorial integrity, independence and national interest, and the democratic, republican and federal system.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Number of governments:<\/strong> 1 federal + 7 provincial + 753 local = <strong>761<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 57<\/strong> divides State power through the five Schedules (table below). The lists hold <strong>35, 21, 25, 22 and 15<\/strong> items — <strong>118<\/strong> in all.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 57 (6):<\/strong> a provincial or local law on a shared subject must not conflict with federal law. <strong>Article 57 (7):<\/strong> a local law must not conflict with provincial law. A conflicting law is void to the extent of the conflict.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 58 — residual power (अवशिष्ट अधिकार):<\/strong> any subject not written in any list belongs to the <strong>Federation<\/strong>. Item 35 of Schedule 5 repeats this.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 59:<\/strong> each level makes its own laws, yearly budget, policies and plans on money matters within its list. Only the <strong>Government of Nepal<\/strong> may take foreign aid and loans (Article 59 (6)).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 137:<\/strong> disputes about jurisdiction between the Federation and a province, between provinces, between a province and a local level, and between local levels go to the <strong>Constitutional Bench<\/strong> of the Supreme Court (the Chief Justice and four other judges).<\/li>\n<li><strong>How federalism came:<\/strong> after the Madhesh movement of 2063 BS the Interim Constitution was amended to promise a federal system (Chapter 2). The Constitution of <strong>3 Ashoj 2072 (20 September 2015)<\/strong> put it into effect. All three levels were first elected in <strong>2074 BS<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>How the local levels were drawn:<\/strong> a commission under Article 295 (3), led by <strong>Balananda Paudel<\/strong>, proposed 719 local levels. The Government put <strong>744<\/strong> into effect on <strong>27 Falgun 2073 (10 March 2017)<\/strong>, replacing the old Village Development Committees and municipalities. The number later became <strong>753<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h6\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>The five lists<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Schedule<\/th>\n<th>Clause of Article 57<\/th>\n<th>Whose power<\/th>\n<th>Items<\/th>\n<th>Examples<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>(1)<\/td>\n<td>Federation alone<\/td>\n<td>35<\/td>\n<td>Defence and army; central bank and monetary policy; foreign affairs and treaties; customs, excise, value-added tax, income tax; citizenship, passport, visa; civil aviation and international airports; national highways and railways; national parks; making of criminal and civil law; atomic energy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>(2)<\/td>\n<td>Province alone<\/td>\n<td>21<\/td>\n<td>Provincial police and peace and order; provincial civil service; Province Public Service Commission; provincial highways; provincial universities and higher education; health services; agriculture and livestock development; land management and land records; management of trusts (गुठी); agro-income tax; trade within the province<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>(3)<\/td>\n<td>Federation and Province together<\/td>\n<td>25<\/td>\n<td>Civil and criminal procedure and evidence; supply and price control of essential goods; prisons and preventive detention; family law (marriage, divorce, adoption, succession); contracts and partnership; drugs and pesticides; planning, family planning and population; trade unions and labour disputes; the professions (law, medicine, engineering, auditing); casino and lottery; tourism, water supply and sanitation; cinema and sports; scientific research<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>(4)<\/td>\n<td>Local level alone<\/td>\n<td>22<\/td>\n<td>Town police; local taxes; basic and secondary education; basic health and sanitation; local market management; local, rural and agricultural roads; local courts, mediation and arbitration; house and land ownership certificates; care of senior citizens and persons with disabilities; records of the unemployed; small hydropower and alternative energy; disaster management<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>(5)<\/td>\n<td>Federation, Province and Local level together<\/td>\n<td>15<\/td>\n<td>Cooperatives; education, health and newspapers; agriculture; electricity, water supply and irrigation; royalty from natural resources and tourism fee; forests, wildlife, environment and biodiversity; mines and minerals; disaster management; social security and poverty alleviation; registration of births, deaths and marriages; archaeology and museums; management of landless squatters; motor vehicle permits<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>One subject, three levels<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Subject<\/th>\n<th>Federation (Schedule 5)<\/th>\n<th>Province (Schedule 6)<\/th>\n<th>Local level (Schedule 8)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Police<\/td>\n<td>Central police, Armed Police Force<\/td>\n<td>Provincial police administration<\/td>\n<td>Town police (नगर प्रहरी)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Education<\/td>\n<td>Central universities; university standards<\/td>\n<td>Provincial universities, higher education<\/td>\n<td>Basic and secondary education<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Health<\/td>\n<td>Health policy and standards; specialised hospitals; control of communicable disease<\/td>\n<td>Health services<\/td>\n<td>Basic health and sanitation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Roads<\/td>\n<td>National highways, railways<\/td>\n<td>Provincial highways<\/td>\n<td>Local, rural and agricultural roads<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Radio and television<\/td>\n<td>Allocation of radio frequency; radio, television, telecommunications, post<\/td>\n<td>Operation of radio, FM and television<\/td>\n<td>Operation of FM<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Statistics<\/td>\n<td>Central statistics<\/td>\n<td>Provincial statistics<\/td>\n<td>Local statistics and records<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Government service<\/td>\n<td>Federal civil service<\/td>\n<td>Provincial civil service<\/td>\n<td>Management of local services<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electricity<\/td>\n<td>Large central projects; transmission lines between provinces<\/td>\n<td>Province-level electricity, irrigation and water supply<\/td>\n<td>Small hydropower, alternative energy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h7\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Part and Articles on the structure of the State<\/td>\n<td>Part 5; Articles 56 to 60<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Levels of government<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Governments in all<\/td>\n<td>761 (1 + 7 + 753)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Items in Schedules 5, 6, 7, 8, 9<\/td>\n<td>35, 21, 25, 22, 15 (total 118)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Residual power<\/td>\n<td>Article 58 — the Federation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disputes about jurisdiction<\/td>\n<td>Article 137 — Constitutional Bench (Chief Justice + 4 judges)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constitution promulgated<\/td>\n<td>3 Ashoj 2072 (20 September 2015)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>744 local levels put into effect<\/td>\n<td>27 Falgun 2073 (10 March 2017)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local levels today<\/td>\n<td>753<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h8\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;5 Federal – 6 Province – 7 both – 8 Local – 9 all three.&quot;<\/strong> Even numbers 6 and 8 belong to the two lower levels alone; 7 and 9 are the shared lists.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Counts: &quot;35 – 21 – 25 – 22 – 15.&quot;<\/strong> The federal list is the longest; the list shared by all three is the shortest.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Three &quot;स&quot; of Article 232:<\/strong> <strong>स<\/strong>हकारिता, <strong>स<\/strong>हअस्तित्व, <strong>स<\/strong>मन्वय.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Left-over goes up&quot;:<\/strong> whatever no list names goes to the Federation (Article 58).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h9\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>district is not a level of government<\/strong>. The District Assembly belongs to the local level (Article 56 (4)) and only coordinates.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Residual power is with the Federation<\/strong>, not with the provinces.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Schedule 7<\/strong> is shared by <strong>two<\/strong> levels; <strong>Schedule 9<\/strong> by <strong>three<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Basic and secondary education<\/strong> is a <strong>local<\/strong> power. Universities are federal or provincial.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Citizenship and passports<\/strong> are federal; so are <strong>customs, excise, value-added tax and income tax<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Disaster management<\/strong> appears in both Schedule 8 and Schedule 9. <strong>Cooperatives<\/strong> appear in Schedules 8 and 9 too. If a question says &quot;concurrent power of all three levels&quot;, the answer is Schedule 9.<\/li>\n<li>The English translation says &quot;State&quot; for a province. Do not confuse it with &quot;the State&quot; meaning the country.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Management of trusts (गुठी)<\/strong> is the last item of the <strong>provincial<\/strong> list.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h10\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<p>Every province is itself one government and contains many local governments.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Districts (Schedule 4)<\/th>\n<th>Local levels<\/th>\n<th>Governments inside the province (1 + local levels)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>14<\/td>\n<td>137<\/td>\n<td>138<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>136<\/td>\n<td>137<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>13<\/td>\n<td>119<\/td>\n<td>120<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<td>85<\/td>\n<td>86<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td>109<\/td>\n<td>110<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<td>79<\/td>\n<td>80<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>88<\/td>\n<td>89<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Totals: 77 districts; 753 local levels; 760 provincial and local governments; with the federal government, 761.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h11\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.1.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which Article of the Constitution says that the State has three levels — Federation, Province and Local level?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Article 50<\/li>\n<li>(b) Article 56<\/li>\n<li>(c) Article 74<\/li>\n<li>(d) Article 232<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Article 56 (1), in Part 5, sets out the three-level structure. Article 232 deals with relations among the levels.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.1.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the Schedule with its list.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Schedule 5 — A. Local level powers<\/li>\n<li>Schedule 6 — B. Concurrent powers of the Federation and the Province<\/li>\n<li>Schedule 7 — C. Federal powers<\/li>\n<li>Schedule 8 — D. Provincial powers<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Schedule 5 is federal, 6 provincial, 7 shared by the Federation and the Province, and 8 local. Schedule 9 is shared by all three.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.1.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>A new subject — for example, the regulation of artificial intelligence — is not named in any of the five lists in Schedules 5 to 9. Under the Constitution, which level may make law on it?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) The Federation<\/li>\n<li>(b) The Province in which the matter arises<\/li>\n<li>(c) The Local level<\/li>\n<li>(d) The Federation and the Province jointly<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — A subject that no list names is a residual power, and Article 58 gives residual power to the Federation.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.1.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consider these statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The District Assembly is part of the local level.<\/li>\n<li>Schedule 9 has 15 items.<\/li>\n<li>Basic and secondary education is in the federal list.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which of them are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Statements 1 and 2 are correct (Article 56 (4); Schedule 9). Basic and secondary education is in Schedule 8, the local list.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.1.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Three of these are in the federal list (Schedule 5). Which one is <strong>not<\/strong>?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Central bank and monetary policy<\/li>\n<li>(b) Management of trusts (गुठी)<\/li>\n<li>(c) Civil aviation and international airports<\/li>\n<li>(d) Customs and excise duty<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Management of trusts is item 21 of the provincial list (Schedule 6).<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h12\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> What are the three tiers of government in Nepal, and how does the Constitution divide power among them?<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> Article 56 of the Constitution makes Nepal a State of three levels: the <strong>Federation<\/strong>, seven <strong>Provinces<\/strong> and 753 <strong>Local levels<\/strong> (rural municipalities and municipalities, with District Assemblies for coordination). Each level has its own executive, its own law-making body and its own budget. <strong>Division of power:<\/strong> Article 57 uses five lists. <strong>Schedule 5<\/strong> (35 items) is for the Federation alone — defence, foreign affairs, currency, the main taxes. <strong>Schedule 6<\/strong> (21 items) is for the Province alone — provincial police, provincial highways, agriculture. <strong>Schedule 8<\/strong> (22 items) is for the Local level alone — basic and secondary education, basic health, local roads. <strong>Schedule 7<\/strong> (25 items) is shared by the Federation and the Province, and <strong>Schedule 9<\/strong> (15 items) by all three. Anything left out belongs to the Federation (Article 58). On shared subjects a lower-level law must not conflict with a higher-level law. Relations among the levels rest on cooperation, coexistence and coordination (Article 232), and disputes about jurisdiction go to the Constitutional Bench of the Supreme Court.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h13\">4.2 Provincial executive, Provincial Assembly and provincial institutions (प्रदेश कार्यपालिका, प्रदेश सभा र प्रदेशका संस्था)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h14\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>The provincial system copies the federal one, but the numbers differ: 35 and 25 years, 20 percent, 15, 30 and 35 days. Examiners use those differences. Names of Chief Ministers and Province Chiefs are regular current-affairs questions.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h15\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>Each province has a small copy of the national system.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>Province Chief<\/strong> (प्रदेश प्रमुख) stands for the Government of Nepal in the province, as the President stands for the nation. The post is mostly ceremonial.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>Chief Minister<\/strong> (मुख्यमन्त्री) and the <strong>Provincial Council of Ministers<\/strong> (प्रदेश मन्त्रिपरिषद्) hold the real executive power.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>Provincial Assembly<\/strong> (प्रदेश सभा) with <strong>one house<\/strong> makes provincial laws and passes the provincial budget. The Chief Minister must keep its confidence.<\/li>\n<li>Each province also has a <strong>High Court<\/strong>, a <strong>Chief Attorney<\/strong> and a <strong>Province Public Service Commission<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h16\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Province Chief — Articles 163 to 167<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Province Chief is the <strong>representative of the Government of Nepal<\/strong> in the province.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Appointed by the President<\/strong> — one for each province. In practice the President acts on the recommendation of the federal Council of Ministers.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Term: five years<\/strong>, unless the President removes the holder earlier. A person <strong>cannot be Province Chief twice in the same province<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Qualification:<\/strong> qualified to be a member of the Federal Parliament, and <strong>35 years<\/strong> of age.<\/li>\n<li>Takes the oath before the <strong>President<\/strong>. If the post falls vacant, the President may ask the Chief of another province to act.<\/li>\n<li>Acts on the recommendation of the Provincial Council of Ministers, given through the Chief Minister (Article 166).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Provincial executive — Articles 162, 168 to 174<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Executive power of the province lies in the <strong>Provincial Council of Ministers<\/strong>. All work is done in the name of the Provincial Government.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 168<\/strong> copies Article 76:\n<ul>\n<li>(1) the leader of the party with a <strong>majority<\/strong> in the Assembly becomes Chief Minister;<\/li>\n<li>(2) if no party has a majority, a member who can command a majority <strong>with the support of two or more parties<\/strong>;<\/li>\n<li>(3) if that fails, the leader of the <strong>largest party<\/strong>;<\/li>\n<li>(5) then any member who shows a ground for winning confidence;<\/li>\n<li>(7) if all fail, the Assembly is dissolved and an election is held <strong>within six months<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li>A Chief Minister appointed under clause (2) or (3) must win a <strong>vote of confidence within 30 days<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The Council of Ministers may have <strong>at most 20 percent<\/strong> of the total members of the Assembly, <strong>including the Chief Minister<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>A person who is not a member may be made a minister, but must become a member <strong>within six months<\/strong> (Article 170).<\/li>\n<li>The Chief Minister and ministers take the oath before the <strong>Province Chief<\/strong>. The Chief Minister keeps the Province Chief informed (Article 173).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Provincial Assembly — Articles 175 to 196<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One house<\/strong> (एक सदनात्मक). Term <strong>five years<\/strong>; during an emergency a provincial Act may extend it by up to one year.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Composition (Article 176):<\/strong> seats filled by first-past-the-post are <strong>twice<\/strong> the number of House of Representatives constituencies in the province. These are counted as <strong>60 percent<\/strong>; the other <strong>40 percent<\/strong> are filled by proportional representation.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Member:<\/strong> citizen, voter of the province, <strong>25 years<\/strong> of age. At least <strong>one third<\/strong> of each party's members must be women.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Speaker and Deputy Speaker<\/strong> (प्रदेश सभामुख, उपसभामुख): elected <strong>within 15 days<\/strong> of the first meeting; one of the two must be a <strong>woman<\/strong>, and they must be from <strong>different parties<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>First session called <strong>within 20 days<\/strong> of the final results. The gap between two sessions may not exceed <strong>six months<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Quorum:<\/strong> one fourth of the total members. A member who is absent from <strong>ten meetings in a row<\/strong> without notice loses the seat.<\/li>\n<li><strong>No-confidence motion (Article 188):<\/strong> moved by <strong>one fourth<\/strong> of the members; it must name the next Chief Minister; it cannot be moved in the <strong>first two years<\/strong> after appointment, nor within <strong>one year<\/strong> of a failed motion.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Law-making and money — Articles 197 to 213<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Assembly makes laws on Schedules 6, 7 and 9.<\/li>\n<li>The Province Chief gives assent to a Bill <strong>within 15 days<\/strong>, and may send back a Bill (not a Money Bill) once.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ordinance (अध्यादेश):<\/strong> issued by the Province Chief on the recommendation of the Council of Ministers when the Assembly is not sitting. It lapses <strong>60 days<\/strong> after the Assembly meets unless passed.<\/li>\n<li>No tax without law. A province may not raise a loan or give a guarantee except as federal law allows (Article 203).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Provincial Consolidated Fund<\/strong> (प्रदेश सञ्चित कोष): Article 204. The provincial Finance Minister presents the yearly estimate (Article 207); federal law fixes the date as <strong>1 Ashad<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Other provincial institutions<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chief Attorney<\/strong> (मुख्य न्यायाधिवक्ता), Article 160: appointed by the Province Chief on the recommendation of the Chief Minister; works under the Attorney General; chief legal adviser of the Provincial Government.<\/li>\n<li><strong>High Court<\/strong> in every province (Chapter 3). <strong>Province Public Service Commission<\/strong> (Article 244; topic 4.6).<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Auditor General<\/strong> of Nepal audits provincial and local accounts as well (Article 241).<\/li>\n<li>A province's <strong>capital<\/strong> and <strong>name<\/strong> are decided by a <strong>two-thirds majority<\/strong> of its Assembly (Articles 288 (2) and 295 (2)).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The present Assemblies<\/strong> 🔄<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Provincial Assemblies were <strong>not<\/strong> dissolved in 2082 BS. They are the ones elected on <strong>4 Mangsir 2079 (20 November 2022)<\/strong>, and their term runs to Mangsir 2084.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Parshuram Khapung<\/strong>, Province Chief of Koshi, was appointed on 23 Kartik 2078, and his five-year term was due to end on <strong>24 Kartik 2083 (10 November 2026)<\/strong>. Reports published in the first days of November 2026 call him the first Province Chief to complete a full term. No report naming a successor was found when this chapter was checked.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h17\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Federation and province compared<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point<\/th>\n<th>Federation<\/th>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ceremonial head<\/td>\n<td>President (elected)<\/td>\n<td>Province Chief (appointed by the President)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum age of the head<\/td>\n<td>45<\/td>\n<td>35<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Head of government<\/td>\n<td>Prime Minister<\/td>\n<td>Chief Minister<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Article on forming the government<\/td>\n<td>76<\/td>\n<td>168<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Size of the Council of Ministers<\/td>\n<td>At most 25<\/td>\n<td>At most 20 percent of the Assembly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legislature<\/td>\n<td>Two houses<\/td>\n<td>One house<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum age of a member<\/td>\n<td>25 (House of Representatives); 35 (National Assembly)<\/td>\n<td>25<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Term<\/td>\n<td>5 years (House of Representatives)<\/td>\n<td>5 years<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assent to a Bill<\/td>\n<td>President, within 15 days<\/td>\n<td>Province Chief, within 15 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinance lapses<\/td>\n<td>60 days after Parliament meets<\/td>\n<td>60 days after the Assembly meets<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No-confidence motion<\/td>\n<td>By one fourth; not in the first two years<\/td>\n<td>By one fourth; not in the first two years<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Province Chiefs and Chief Ministers<\/strong> 🔄 (as reported up to early November 2026)<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>First Chief Minister (2074)<\/th>\n<th>Chief Minister now<\/th>\n<th>Province Chief now<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Sher Dhan Rai<\/td>\n<td>Hikmat Kumar Karki (CPN-UML)<\/td>\n<td>Parshuram Khapung's five-year term was due to end on 24 Kartik 2083 (10 November 2026); no report of a successor was found when checked<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>Lalbabu Raut<\/td>\n<td>Krishna Prasad Yadav (Nepali Congress)<\/td>\n<td>Surendra Labh Karna<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Dormani Paudel<\/td>\n<td>Kiran Thapa Magar (CPN-UML), appointed 4 Bhadra 2083 (20 August 2026) under Article 168 (2)<\/td>\n<td>Deepak Prasad Devkota<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>Prithvi Subba Gurung<\/td>\n<td>Surendra Raj Pandey (Nepali Congress)<\/td>\n<td>Dilli Raj Bhatta<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>Shankar Pokharel<\/td>\n<td>Chet Narayan Acharya (CPN-UML)<\/td>\n<td>Krishna Bahadur Gharti Magar<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>Mahendra Bahadur Shahi<\/td>\n<td>Mangal Bahadur Shahi (Nepali Communist Party), appointed in Shrawan 2083 (late July 2026) after Yam Lal Kandel resigned on 9 Shrawan 2083 (25 July 2026)<\/td>\n<td>Yagya Raj Joshi<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Trilochan Bhatta<\/td>\n<td>Khagaraj Bhatta (Nepali Communist Party), appointed 29 Shrawan 2083 (14 August 2026) under Article 168 (2)<\/td>\n<td>Najir Miya<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Provincial governments change often. Check these names again close to your examination.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h18\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Articles on the Province Chief<\/td>\n<td>163 to 167<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum age of the Province Chief<\/td>\n<td>35<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Term of the Province Chief<\/td>\n<td>5 years; not twice in the same province<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Article on forming the provincial government<\/td>\n<td>168<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Largest size of the Council of Ministers<\/td>\n<td>20 percent of the Assembly, including the Chief Minister<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vote of confidence<\/td>\n<td>Within 30 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whole process of appointing a Chief Minister<\/td>\n<td>Within 35 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum age of an Assembly member<\/td>\n<td>25<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First-past-the-post : proportional seats<\/td>\n<td>60 : 40<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speaker elected<\/td>\n<td>Within 15 days of the first meeting<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First session called<\/td>\n<td>Within 20 days of the final results<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quorum<\/td>\n<td>One fourth<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assent to a Bill<\/td>\n<td>Within 15 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinance lapses<\/td>\n<td>60 days after the Assembly meets<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seats in the seven Assemblies<\/td>\n<td>550 (330 + 220)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Last Provincial Assembly elections<\/td>\n<td>4 Mangsir 2079 (20 November 2022)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h19\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ages &quot;35 – 25 – 21&quot;:<\/strong> Province Chief 35, Assembly member 25, local representative 21.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Days &quot;15 – 20 – 30 – 35&quot;:<\/strong> Speaker in 15, first session in 20, confidence vote in 30, Chief Minister process in 35.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Twice and sixty&quot;:<\/strong> first-past-the-post seats are <strong>twice<\/strong> the province's House constituencies, and they are <strong>sixty<\/strong> percent of the Assembly.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;168 is 76 in the province.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h20\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The Province Chief is <strong>appointed, not elected<\/strong>, and is <strong>not<\/strong> the head of the provincial government.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Province Chief<\/strong> appoints the Chief Minister; the <strong>President<\/strong> appoints the Province Chief.<\/li>\n<li>The 20 percent limit <strong>includes<\/strong> the Chief Minister.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Chief Attorney<\/strong> (province) is not the <strong>Attorney General<\/strong> (federation).<\/li>\n<li>A province fixes its own capital and name by <strong>two thirds of its Assembly<\/strong>; the Federal Parliament does not vote on it.<\/li>\n<li>A Provincial Assembly has <strong>no upper house<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Ministers of State take the oath before the <strong>Chief Minister<\/strong>; ministers before the <strong>Province Chief<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The news often says &quot;Governor&quot; for the Province Chief. The Constitution's word is प्रदेश प्रमुख.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h21\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<p>The figures follow from Article 176 and Article 168 (9). Majority means more than half of all seats.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Assembly seats (first-past-the-post + proportional)<\/th>\n<th>Majority<\/th>\n<th>Two thirds<\/th>\n<th>Largest Council of Ministers (20%)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>93 (56 + 37)<\/td>\n<td>47<\/td>\n<td>62<\/td>\n<td>18<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>107 (64 + 43)<\/td>\n<td>54<\/td>\n<td>72<\/td>\n<td>21<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>110 (66 + 44)<\/td>\n<td>56<\/td>\n<td>74<\/td>\n<td>22<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>60 (36 + 24)<\/td>\n<td>31<\/td>\n<td>40<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>87 (52 + 35)<\/td>\n<td>44<\/td>\n<td>58<\/td>\n<td>17<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>40 (24 + 16)<\/td>\n<td>21<\/td>\n<td>27<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>53 (32 + 21)<\/td>\n<td>27<\/td>\n<td>36<\/td>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Totals: 550 seats — 330 first-past-the-post and 220 proportional.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h22\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.2.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who appoints the Province Chief?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) The Prime Minister<\/li>\n<li>(b) The Chief Minister<\/li>\n<li>(c) The President<\/li>\n<li>(d) The Provincial Assembly<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Article 163 (2): the President appoints one Province Chief for each province.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.2.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>What is the largest size allowed for a Provincial Council of Ministers?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Twenty percent of the total members of the Provincial Assembly, including the Chief Minister<\/li>\n<li>(b) Twenty-five members, including the Chief Minister<\/li>\n<li>(c) Ten percent of the total members of the Provincial Assembly<\/li>\n<li>(d) Fifteen percent of the members, not counting the Chief Minister<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Article 168 (9). The limit of 25 applies to the federal Council of Ministers.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.2.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consider these statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>A Provincial Assembly has one house.<\/li>\n<li>A member of a Provincial Assembly must be at least 25 years old.<\/li>\n<li>A Province Chief must be at least 35 years old.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which of them are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — All three are correct (Articles 175, 178 and 164).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.2.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Fill in the blank. A Chief Minister appointed under Article 168 (2) must obtain a vote of confidence from the Provincial Assembly within ______ days of appointment.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 15<\/li>\n<li>(b) 30<\/li>\n<li>(c) 35<\/li>\n<li>(d) 60<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Article 168 (4) gives 30 days. Thirty-five days is the limit for the whole process of appointing a Chief Minister.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.2.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the office with its role.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Province Chief — A. Chief legal adviser of the Provincial Government<\/li>\n<li>Chief Minister — B. Representative of the Government of Nepal in the province<\/li>\n<li>Chief Attorney — C. Head of the Provincial Council of Ministers<\/li>\n<li>Speaker — D. Presides over the Provincial Assembly<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Articles 163, 168, 160 and 182.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h23\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> How is a provincial government formed under the Constitution? Describe the role of the Province Chief in it.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> Article 168 sets out the steps. (1) The Province Chief appoints as <strong>Chief Minister<\/strong> the leader of the party that has a majority in the Provincial Assembly. (2) If no party has a majority, the Province Chief appoints a member who can command a majority with the support of two or more parties. (3) If that is not possible within 30 days of the final results, or the person fails the confidence vote, the leader of the largest party is appointed. A Chief Minister appointed under (2) or (3) must win a <strong>vote of confidence within 30 days<\/strong>. (4) If this also fails, any member who shows a ground for winning confidence may be appointed. If no government can be formed, the Assembly is dissolved and an election is held within six months. The Chief Minister then forms a <strong>Council of Ministers<\/strong> of at most <strong>20 percent<\/strong> of the Assembly's members, on inclusive lines. <strong>Role of the Province Chief:<\/strong> appointed by the President as the representative of the Government of Nepal, the Province Chief appoints the Chief Minister and ministers, gives them the oath, summons the Assembly, assents to Bills and issues ordinances — but acts on the recommendation of the Council of Ministers. The post is ceremonial; the Chief Minister governs.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h24\">4.3 Local government: structure, powers and the Local Government Operation Act (स्थानीय सरकार: संरचना, अधिकार र स्थानीय सरकार सञ्चालन ऐन, २०७४)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h25\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>The local level is the government closest to the citizen, and the office where many of you will work. Its numbers — 4 and 2, 5 and 3, 3, 9, 21 — are easy to set as questions. Koshi's official model question on the number of local levels shows the style.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h26\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>Nepal has <strong>753 local levels<\/strong>. A local level is either a <strong>rural municipality<\/strong> (गाउँपालिका) or a <strong>municipality<\/strong> (नगरपालिका); large municipalities are called sub-metropolitan or metropolitan cities. Each is divided into <strong>wards<\/strong> (वडा).<\/p>\n<p>A local level is a full government in small form:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>an <strong>executive<\/strong> — the Village Executive (गाउँ कार्यपालिका) or Municipal Executive (नगर कार्यपालिका);<\/li>\n<li>a law-making <strong>assembly<\/strong> — the Village Assembly (गाउँ सभा) or Municipal Assembly (नगर सभा);<\/li>\n<li>a <strong>Judicial Committee<\/strong> (न्यायिक समिति) that settles small disputes.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>In each district the heads of the local levels meet as a <strong>District Assembly<\/strong> (जिल्ला सभा), which elects a <strong>District Coordination Committee<\/strong> (जिल्ला समन्वय समिति). It coordinates; it does not govern.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>Local Government Operation Act, 2074<\/strong> (स्थानीय सरकार सञ्चालन ऐन, २०७४) turns the short lists of Schedules 8 and 9 into detailed duties.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h27\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>In the Constitution<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Part 17<\/strong> (Articles 214–220) Local Executive; <strong>Part 18<\/strong> (Articles 221–227) Local Legislature; <strong>Part 19<\/strong> (Articles 228–230) Local Financial Procedures.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Numbers:<\/strong> 753 local levels = 6 metropolitan cities + 11 sub-metropolitan cities + 276 municipalities + 460 rural municipalities; <strong>6,743 wards<\/strong> (Chapter 1).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Head and deputy:<\/strong> a rural municipality has a <strong>Chairperson<\/strong> (अध्यक्ष) and <strong>Vice-Chairperson<\/strong> (उपाध्यक्ष); a municipality has a <strong>Mayor<\/strong> (प्रमुख) and <strong>Deputy Mayor<\/strong> (उपप्रमुख). Voters elect them directly by first-past-the-post.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Village Executive (Article 215):<\/strong> Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, all ward chairpersons, <strong>4 women<\/strong> members and <strong>2 members from Dalit or minority communities<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Municipal Executive (Article 216):<\/strong> Mayor, Deputy Mayor, all ward chairpersons, <strong>5 women<\/strong> members and <strong>3 members from Dalit or minority communities<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>These extra members are chosen by the Assembly <strong>within 15 days<\/strong> of the final election results.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ward Committee (Articles 222, 223):<\/strong> a ward chairperson and <strong>4 members<\/strong>. Each ward must send at least <strong>2 women<\/strong> to the Assembly.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Assembly:<\/strong> the head, the deputy, the ward chairpersons, the four members from every ward and the Dalit or minority members of the Executive. The head and deputy of the Executive are also the chair and vice-chair of the Assembly (Article 224) — there is no separate Speaker.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Minimum age<\/strong> for any local post: <strong>21<\/strong>. <strong>Term: 5 years.<\/strong> A person elected head for two terms cannot stand again. The next election must be held <strong>within six months<\/strong> of the end of the term (Article 225).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Judicial Committee (Article 217):<\/strong> <strong>3 members<\/strong>. The <strong>Vice-Chairperson<\/strong> or <strong>Deputy Mayor<\/strong> coordinates it; the Assembly elects the other two from among its members.<\/li>\n<li><strong>District Assembly and District Coordination Committee (Article 220):<\/strong>\n<ul>\n<li>the District Assembly consists of the Chairpersons and Vice-Chairpersons of the rural municipalities and the Mayors and Deputy Mayors of the municipalities in the district;<\/li>\n<li>it elects a District Coordination Committee of <strong>at most 9 members<\/strong>: a Chief, a Deputy Chief, at least <strong>3 women<\/strong> and at least <strong>1<\/strong> Dalit or minority member;<\/li>\n<li>term 5 years; a person elected to the Committee gives up the seat in the Village or Municipal Assembly;<\/li>\n<li>work: coordinate the local levels; monitor development work for balance; coordinate between federal and provincial offices and the local levels in the district.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>Law-making:<\/strong> the Assemblies make laws on Schedules 8 and 9 (Article 226).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Money:<\/strong> no tax except by law (Article 228); every local level has a <strong>Local Consolidated Fund<\/strong> (स्थानीय सञ्चित कोष, Article 229); the Executive presents the yearly estimate to the Assembly (Article 230). Federal law fixes the date as <strong>10 Ashad<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>In the Local Government Operation Act, 2074<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Authenticated on <strong>29 Ashoj 2074 (15 October 2017)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Wards:<\/strong> a rural municipality has <strong>5 to 21<\/strong> wards; a municipality <strong>9 to 35<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Classification of municipalities (section 8):<\/strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>sub-metropolitan city<\/strong> — at least <strong>200,000<\/strong> permanent residents and an average yearly internal income of at least <strong>Rs 250 million<\/strong> (25 crore) over the last five years;<\/li>\n<li><strong>metropolitan city<\/strong> — at least <strong>500,000<\/strong> permanent residents and at least <strong>Rs 1 billion<\/strong> (1 arab);<\/li>\n<li>both must also meet standards for roads, hospitals, water supply and other city services.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>Meetings:<\/strong> the Executive meets at least <strong>once a month<\/strong>; the Assembly normally meets <strong>twice a year<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Judicial Committee (section 47):<\/strong> it may <strong>decide 13 kinds<\/strong> of dispute and may settle <strong>11 more kinds only through mediation<\/strong> (मेलमिलाप) — 24 in all. It must first try to bring the two sides to agreement, and may set up mediation centres in the wards.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Chief Administrative Officer<\/strong> (प्रमुख प्रशासकीय अधिकृत) heads the staff of a local level.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Elections<\/strong> (see also topic 4.5)<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>2074 BS:<\/strong> three phases — 31 Baisakh (14 May 2017), 14 Ashad (28 June 2017) and 2 Ashoj (18 September 2017). They were the first local elections in about twenty years.<\/li>\n<li><strong>2079 BS:<\/strong> one phase — <strong>30 Baisakh 2079 (13 May 2022)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Next<\/strong> 🔄: the present term ends in Baisakh 2084 (May 2027).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Posts filled by direct election:<\/strong> 753 heads + 753 deputies + 6,743 ward chairpersons + 26,972 ward members = <strong>35,221<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>🔄 In September 2026 the Government was reported to be preparing rules for merging local levels and reviewing their classification after the next election.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h28\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Rural municipality and municipality compared<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point<\/th>\n<th>Rural municipality (गाउँपालिका)<\/th>\n<th>Municipality (नगरपालिका)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Number<\/td>\n<td>460<\/td>\n<td>293 (276 + 11 sub-metropolitan + 6 metropolitan)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Head<\/td>\n<td>Chairperson (अध्यक्ष)<\/td>\n<td>Mayor (प्रमुख)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deputy<\/td>\n<td>Vice-Chairperson (उपाध्यक्ष)<\/td>\n<td>Deputy Mayor (उपप्रमुख)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Executive<\/td>\n<td>Village Executive<\/td>\n<td>Municipal Executive<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Women members added to the Executive<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dalit or minority members added<\/td>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assembly<\/td>\n<td>Village Assembly<\/td>\n<td>Municipal Assembly<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coordinator of the Judicial Committee<\/td>\n<td>Vice-Chairperson<\/td>\n<td>Deputy Mayor<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of wards<\/td>\n<td>5 to 21<\/td>\n<td>9 to 35<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Article<\/td>\n<td>215<\/td>\n<td>216<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Bodies at the local level<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Body<\/th>\n<th>Members<\/th>\n<th>Article<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ward Committee<\/td>\n<td>Ward chairperson + 4 members<\/td>\n<td>222, 223<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Village or Municipal Executive<\/td>\n<td>Head, deputy, ward chairpersons, women members, Dalit or minority members<\/td>\n<td>215, 216<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Village or Municipal Assembly<\/td>\n<td>Head, deputy, ward chairpersons, 4 members from each ward, Dalit or minority members of the Executive<\/td>\n<td>222, 223<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Judicial Committee<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>217<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>District Assembly<\/td>\n<td>Heads and deputies of all local levels in the district<\/td>\n<td>220<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>District Coordination Committee<\/td>\n<td>At most 9<\/td>\n<td>220<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h29\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Local levels / wards<\/td>\n<td>753 / 6,743<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metropolitan / sub-metropolitan / municipality / rural municipality<\/td>\n<td>6 / 11 / 276 / 460<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extra members of a Village Executive<\/td>\n<td>4 women + 2 Dalit or minority<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extra members of a Municipal Executive<\/td>\n<td>5 women + 3 Dalit or minority<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ward Committee<\/td>\n<td>1 chairperson + 4 members<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Judicial Committee<\/td>\n<td>3 members<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>District Coordination Committee<\/td>\n<td>At most 9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum age; term<\/td>\n<td>21; 5 years<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local Government Operation Act authenticated<\/td>\n<td>29 Ashoj 2074 (15 October 2017)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wards in a rural municipality / municipality<\/td>\n<td>5–21 / 9–35<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sub-metropolitan city<\/td>\n<td>200,000 residents; Rs 250 million income<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metropolitan city<\/td>\n<td>500,000 residents; Rs 1 billion income<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disputes before the Judicial Committee<\/td>\n<td>13 to decide + 11 to mediate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local elections<\/td>\n<td>2074 (three phases); 30 Baisakh 2079 (13 May 2022)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Directly elected local posts<\/td>\n<td>35,221<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h30\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;Village 4 + 2, Town 5 + 3.&quot;<\/strong> The town always gets one more.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;3 – 5 – 9&quot;:<\/strong> Judicial Committee 3; Ward Committee 5 (1 + 4); District Coordination Committee at most 9.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;The deputy is the judge&quot;:<\/strong> the Vice-Chairperson or Deputy Mayor leads the Judicial Committee.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;2 lakh sub, 5 lakh metro.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;13 decide, 11 mediate.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h31\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>municipality<\/strong> adds <strong>5<\/strong> women; a <strong>rural municipality<\/strong> adds <strong>4<\/strong>. Examiners swap them.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Judicial Committee<\/strong> is led by the <strong>deputy<\/strong>, not the head.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>District Coordination Committee<\/strong> has <strong>at most 9<\/strong> members and is elected by the <strong>District Assembly<\/strong>, not by the voters.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Chief District Officer<\/strong> is a federal civil servant, not a member of the District Coordination Committee.<\/li>\n<li>The head of a municipality is called <strong>प्रमुख<\/strong> in Nepali; &quot;Mayor&quot; is the English word.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>minimum age is 21<\/strong> at the local level, but 25 for the Provincial Assembly.<\/li>\n<li>The local elections of <strong>2074<\/strong> were held in <strong>three<\/strong> phases; those of <strong>2079<\/strong> in <strong>one<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>753<\/strong>, not 744 — 744 was the first number, in Falgun 2073.<\/li>\n<li>The local Assembly has <strong>no Speaker<\/strong>; the head of the Executive chairs it.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h32\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Local levels<\/th>\n<th>Wards<\/th>\n<th>District Coordination Committees<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>137<\/td>\n<td>1,157<\/td>\n<td>14<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>136<\/td>\n<td>1,271<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>119<\/td>\n<td>1,121<\/td>\n<td>13<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>85<\/td>\n<td>759<\/td>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>109<\/td>\n<td>983<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>79<\/td>\n<td>718<\/td>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>88<\/td>\n<td>734<\/td>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Totals: 753 local levels; 6,743 wards; 77 District Coordination Committees. Madhesh has the most wards; Karnali the fewest.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h33\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.3.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who coordinates the Judicial Committee of a municipality?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) The Mayor<\/li>\n<li>(b) The Chief Administrative Officer<\/li>\n<li>(c) The senior-most ward chairperson<\/li>\n<li>(d) The Deputy Mayor<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Article 217: the Deputy Mayor in a municipality and the Vice-Chairperson in a rural municipality.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.3.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>What is the largest number of members a District Coordination Committee may have?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 7<\/li>\n<li>(b) 9<\/li>\n<li>(c) 11<\/li>\n<li>(d) 15<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Article 220 (3): at most nine, including the Chief and the Deputy Chief.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.3.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which of these statements is <strong>not<\/strong> correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A Ward Committee has a chairperson and four members.<\/li>\n<li>(b) The term of a Village Assembly and a Municipal Assembly is five years.<\/li>\n<li>(c) A Municipal Executive includes four women members elected by the Municipal Assembly.<\/li>\n<li>(d) A candidate for a local post must be at least 21 years old.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — A Municipal Executive includes <strong>five<\/strong> women members (Article 216). Four is the number for a Village Executive.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.3.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the types of local level from the most numerous to the least numerous.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Metropolitan cities<\/li>\n<li>Rural municipalities<\/li>\n<li>Sub-metropolitan cities<\/li>\n<li>Municipalities<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2 – 4 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<li>(b) 4 – 2 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 – 4 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2 – 3 – 4 – 1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Rural municipalities 460, municipalities 276, sub-metropolitan cities 11, metropolitan cities 6.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.3.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Under the Local Government Operation Act, 2074, how many permanent residents must a town have, at the least, to become a sub-metropolitan city?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 100,000<\/li>\n<li>(b) 150,000<\/li>\n<li>(c) 200,000<\/li>\n<li>(d) 500,000<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Two hundred thousand (2 lakh). Five hundred thousand is the figure for a metropolitan city.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h34\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> Describe the structure of a local level in Nepal and name its main organs.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> A local level is a rural municipality or a municipality, divided into wards. <strong>(1) Ward Committee:<\/strong> a ward chairperson and four members, directly elected. <strong>(2) Executive:<\/strong> the Village Executive is led by a Chairperson and Vice-Chairperson, the Municipal Executive by a Mayor and Deputy Mayor, all directly elected. It includes all ward chairpersons, and women and Dalit or minority members chosen by the Assembly — four and two in a rural municipality, five and three in a municipality. It carries out the powers in Schedules 8 and 9. <strong>(3) Assembly:<\/strong> the Village or Municipal Assembly consists of the head, the deputy, the ward chairpersons, the ward members and the Dalit or minority members of the Executive. It passes local laws and the yearly budget. <strong>(4) Judicial Committee:<\/strong> three members under the deputy head; it decides or mediates small disputes named in the Local Government Operation Act, 2074. <strong>(5) At district level,<\/strong> the heads and deputies form a District Assembly, which elects a District Coordination Committee of at most nine members to coordinate and monitor. The minimum age is 21 and the term is five years.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h35\">4.4 Inter-governmental relations and fiscal federalism (अन्तर-सरकारी सम्बन्ध र वित्तीय संघीयता)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h36\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>Karnali's syllabus names the &quot;revenue jurisdiction of the three tiers&quot; inside its economy unit. The sharing ratios 70 : 15 : 15 and 50 : 25 : 25, the four grants and the budget dates make clean one-line questions. Budget figures come up in current affairs every year.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h37\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>Three levels of government must not pull in three directions. The Constitution gives them rules for working together — <strong>inter-governmental relations<\/strong> (अन्तर-सरकारी सम्बन्ध) — and rules for dividing money — <strong>fiscal federalism<\/strong> (वित्तीय संघीयता).<\/p>\n<p>The money problem is simple to state. The big taxes (customs, value-added tax, income tax) belong to the Federation, but much of the work (schools, health posts, local roads) belongs to the provinces and local levels. So money must flow down. It flows in two ways: <strong>revenue sharing<\/strong> (राजस्व बाँडफाँट), where a fixed share of certain taxes is handed over, and <strong>grants<\/strong> (अनुदान). An independent body, the <strong>National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission<\/strong> (राष्ट्रिय प्राकृतिक स्रोत तथा वित्त आयोग), recommends how much each government should get.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h38\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Relations — Part 20, Articles 231 to 237<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Article 232 (1):<\/strong> relations among the three levels rest on <strong>cooperation, coexistence and coordination<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 232 (2):<\/strong> the Government of Nepal may give directions to a Provincial Council of Ministers on matters of national importance and on matters to be coordinated among provinces.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 232 (3):<\/strong> if an act that seriously harms Nepal's sovereignty, territorial integrity, nationality or independence takes place in a province, the <strong>President<\/strong> may warn the Provincial Council of Ministers, or <strong>suspend or dissolve<\/strong> it and the Assembly <strong>for up to six months<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 232 (4):<\/strong> the Federal Parliament must ratify this <strong>within 35 days<\/strong>. ⚖️ The Nepali text read for this book requires a <strong>majority<\/strong> of all existing members; the English translation read says two thirds. Follow the Nepali text.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 232 (5)–(7):<\/strong> if a dissolution is ratified, a new Assembly is elected <strong>within six months<\/strong>; meanwhile there is <strong>federal rule<\/strong> (संघीय शासन) in the province and the Federal Parliament may make laws on Schedule 6.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 232 (8):<\/strong> the Government of Nepal may help and direct a Village or Municipal Executive, itself or through the Provincial Government.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 231 (3):<\/strong> if two or more provinces ask, the Federal Parliament may make a law on a Schedule 6 subject for those provinces.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 233:<\/strong> provinces help one another and give equal treatment to residents of other provinces.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 234 — Inter-Province Council (अन्तर प्रदेश परिषद्):<\/strong> settles <strong>political disputes<\/strong> between the Federation and a province and between provinces. Members: the <strong>Prime Minister<\/strong> (chair), the <strong>Home Minister<\/strong>, the <strong>Finance Minister<\/strong> and the <strong>Chief Ministers<\/strong> of the provinces concerned.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 235:<\/strong> the Federal Parliament makes a law for coordination; a Provincial Assembly may settle political disputes between the province and its local levels, working with the District Coordination Committee.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 236:<\/strong> no province or local level may block, tax or discriminate against goods and services moving to another.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The coordination Act:<\/strong> the <strong>Federation, Province and Local Level (Coordination and Inter-relation) Act, 2077<\/strong> creates a <strong>National Coordination Council<\/strong> (राष्ट्रिय समन्वय परिषद्) chaired by the <strong>Prime Minister<\/strong> and a <strong>Province Coordination Council<\/strong> (प्रदेश समन्वय परिषद्) chaired by the <strong>Chief Minister<\/strong> in each province.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Inter-Governmental Fiscal Council<\/strong> (अन्तर-सरकारी वित्त परिषद्) is chaired by the federal <strong>Finance Minister<\/strong> and includes the provincial finance ministers and local representatives.<\/li>\n<li>🔄 <strong>Koshi Declaration, 2083:<\/strong> the Chief Ministers met at Biratnagar on 27–28 Ashoj 2083 (13–14 October 2026). They asked for equalisation grants to be paid in full, for the Federal Civil Service Bill to be passed quickly and for police to be handed to the provinces, and set up an Inter-Province Chief Ministers' Partnership Forum.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Money — Articles 59 and 60, and the Inter-Governmental Fiscal Arrangement Act, 2074<\/strong> (अन्तर-सरकारी वित्त व्यवस्थापन ऐन, २०७४)<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Article 60 (1):<\/strong> each level may levy taxes on subjects in its own list.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 60 (3):<\/strong> the amount of fiscal transfer to provinces and local levels is fixed on the <strong>recommendation of the National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Revenue sharing:<\/strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>value-added tax<\/strong> and <strong>excise on domestic products<\/strong> go into a Federal Divisible Fund and are shared <strong>70 percent Federation : 15 percent provinces : 15 percent local levels<\/strong>;<\/li>\n<li><strong>royalty from natural resources<\/strong> (mountaineering, electricity, forests, mines and minerals, water) is shared <strong>50 percent Federation : 25 percent the province concerned : 25 percent the local level concerned<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>Single tax administration<\/strong> (एकल कर प्रशासन) for taxes that appear in both the provincial and the local list:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>vehicle tax<\/strong> — the province fixes the rate and collects it; shared <strong>60 province : 40 local<\/strong>;<\/li>\n<li><strong>house and land registration fee<\/strong> and <strong>entertainment tax<\/strong> — the province fixes the rate, the local level collects; shared <strong>60 local : 40 province<\/strong>;<\/li>\n<li><strong>advertisement tax<\/strong> — the local level fixes the rate and collects it; shared <strong>60 local : 40 province<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>Four grants (Article 60 (4)–(6)):<\/strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>fiscal equalisation grant<\/strong> (वित्तीय समानीकरण अनुदान) — based on the need for spending and the capacity to raise revenue; free to use;<\/li>\n<li><strong>conditional grant<\/strong> (सशर्त अनुदान) — tied to a named programme or standard;<\/li>\n<li><strong>complementary grant<\/strong> (समपूरक अनुदान) — matching money for infrastructure projects;<\/li>\n<li><strong>special grant<\/strong> (विशेष अनुदान) — for a specific purpose such as basic services, balanced development or the uplift of disadvantaged groups.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li>Each province also gives equalisation grants to its own local levels (Article 60 (5)).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Borrowing:<\/strong> only the Government of Nepal may take <strong>foreign<\/strong> loans and aid. Provinces and local levels may raise <strong>internal<\/strong> loans within the limit recommended by the Commission and with the consent of the Government of Nepal.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Budget days:<\/strong> Federation <strong>15 Jestha<\/strong>; province <strong>1 Ashad<\/strong>; local level <strong>10 Ashad<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Three funds:<\/strong> Federal Consolidated Fund (Article 116), Provincial Consolidated Fund (Article 204), Local Consolidated Fund (Article 229).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission — Articles 250 and 251<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>At most 5 members<\/strong>, including the Chairperson; appointed by the President on the recommendation of the Constitutional Council.<\/li>\n<li>Term <strong>6 years<\/strong>; leaves at age <strong>65<\/strong>; minimum age <strong>45<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Work: fixes the basis for sharing revenue; recommends equalisation grants; studies the basis for conditional grants; recommends the <strong>ceiling for internal loans<\/strong> of all three levels; fixes how investment in and returns from natural resources are shared; studies disputes over natural resources.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Fiscal year 2083/84<\/strong> 🔄<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Federal budget: <strong>Rs 2,124.34 billion<\/strong> (21 kharba 24 arba 34 crore), presented on <strong>15 Jestha 2083 (29 May 2026)<\/strong> by Finance Minister <strong>Dr Swarnim Wagle<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Grants to provinces and local levels: about <strong>Rs 424 billion<\/strong> (table below). Revenue sharing was estimated at a further <strong>Rs 175 billion<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Loan ceiling recommended by the Commission for provinces and local levels: up to <strong>12 percent<\/strong> of their shared revenue plus their own revenue.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h39\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Revenue jurisdiction of the three tiers (from the Schedules)<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level<\/th>\n<th>Taxes and fees named in its list<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Federation (Schedule 5)<\/td>\n<td>Customs; excise; value-added tax; corporate income tax; personal income tax; remuneration tax; passport fee; visa fee; tourism fee; service charges; penalties<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Province (Schedule 6)<\/td>\n<td>House and land registration fee; motor vehicle tax; entertainment tax; advertisement tax; tourism; agro-income tax; service charges; penalties<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local level (Schedule 8)<\/td>\n<td>Property tax; house rent tax; house and land registration fee; motor vehicle tax; business tax; land tax (मालपोत); advertisement tax; entertainment tax; tourism fee; service charges; penalties<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All three (Schedule 9)<\/td>\n<td>Service fees, penalties and royalty from natural resources; tourism fee<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Sharing ratios<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Revenue<\/th>\n<th>Federation<\/th>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Local level<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Value-added tax and domestic excise<\/td>\n<td>70%<\/td>\n<td>15%<\/td>\n<td>15%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Royalty from natural resources<\/td>\n<td>50%<\/td>\n<td>25%<\/td>\n<td>25%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vehicle tax<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>60%<\/td>\n<td>40%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>House and land registration fee; entertainment tax; advertisement tax<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>40%<\/td>\n<td>60%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Grants in the federal budget for 2083/84<\/strong> 🔄 (Rs billion, as announced in the budget speech)<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Grant<\/th>\n<th>To provinces<\/th>\n<th>To local levels<\/th>\n<th>Total<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fiscal equalisation<\/td>\n<td>61.50<\/td>\n<td>90.20<\/td>\n<td>151.70<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conditional<\/td>\n<td>39.72<\/td>\n<td>206.08<\/td>\n<td>245.80<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complementary<\/td>\n<td>4.60<\/td>\n<td>8.93<\/td>\n<td>13.53<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special<\/td>\n<td>3.82<\/td>\n<td>9.40<\/td>\n<td>13.22<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>All four<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>109.64<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>314.61<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>424.25<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The Ministry of Finance's detailed statement, published a few days later, puts the total at Rs 424.28 billion.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h40\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Part and Articles on relations among the levels<\/td>\n<td>Part 20; Articles 231 to 237<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Three bases of relations<\/td>\n<td>Cooperation, coexistence, coordination — Article 232<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Suspension or dissolution of a provincial government<\/td>\n<td>Up to 6 months; ratified within 35 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inter-Province Council<\/td>\n<td>Article 234; chaired by the Prime Minister<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value-added tax and domestic excise<\/td>\n<td>70 : 15 : 15<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Royalty from natural resources<\/td>\n<td>50 : 25 : 25<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vehicle tax, province : local<\/td>\n<td>60 : 40<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kinds of grant<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commission members<\/td>\n<td>At most 5; term 6 years; Articles 250–251<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Budget days<\/td>\n<td>15 Jestha; 1 Ashad; 10 Ashad<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Federal budget 2083/84 🔄<\/td>\n<td>Rs 2,124.34 billion<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grants 2083/84 🔄<\/td>\n<td>About Rs 424 billion<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coordination Act<\/td>\n<td>2077 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inter-Governmental Fiscal Arrangement Act<\/td>\n<td>2074 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h41\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;70 – 15 – 15 for tax; 50 – 25 – 25 for nature.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Whoever collects keeps sixty.&quot;<\/strong> The province collects vehicle tax and keeps 60; the local level collects the other three and keeps 60.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Four grants &quot;E – C – C – S&quot;:<\/strong> Equalisation, Conditional, Complementary, Special.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Budget days step down by level:<\/strong> Jestha 15 → Ashad 1 → Ashad 10.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Inter-Province Council = &quot;PM + Home + Finance + Chief Ministers&quot;.<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h42\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>Inter-Province Council<\/strong> settles <strong>political<\/strong> disputes. Disputes about <strong>jurisdiction<\/strong> go to the <strong>Constitutional Bench<\/strong> (Article 137).<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>equalisation<\/strong> grant is the free one; the <strong>conditional<\/strong> grant is tied. In money terms the conditional grant is the largest.<\/li>\n<li><strong>70 : 15 : 15<\/strong> applies only to value-added tax and domestic excise — not to customs or income tax, which stay with the Federation.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Foreign loans:<\/strong> the Federation only. A province needs the &quot;consent of the Centre&quot; even for foreign grants (Schedule 6, item 2).<\/li>\n<li>The Commission <strong>recommends<\/strong>; the Government of Nepal <strong>distributes<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The Commission has <strong>at most five<\/strong> members — like most constitutional commissions — but its members need <strong>20 years'<\/strong> experience in the field.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Royalty<\/strong> is shared with the province and local level <strong>concerned<\/strong>, not with all.<\/li>\n<li>The Federal Parliament, not the President alone, has the last word on suspending a provincial government.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h43\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<p>Grants from the Federation to each provincial government for 2083/84 🔄 (all four grants together, from the Ministry of Finance's statement as reported).<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Grants for 2083/84 (Rs billion)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>16.47<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>17.60<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>15.21<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>12.73<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>14.42<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>17.21<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>16.01<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Total: Rs 109.65 billion. <strong>Madhesh<\/strong> receives the most and <strong>Gandaki<\/strong> the least. In the equalisation grant alone, <strong>Karnali<\/strong> receives the most (Rs 10.63 billion) and Gandaki the least (Rs 7.85 billion).<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h44\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.4.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who chairs the Inter-Province Council under Article 234?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) The Prime Minister<\/li>\n<li>(b) The President<\/li>\n<li>(c) The Home Minister<\/li>\n<li>(d) The Chairperson of the National Assembly<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — The Prime Minister chairs it; the Home Minister, the Finance Minister and the Chief Ministers concerned are members.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.4.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Fill in the blank. Value-added tax and excise on domestic products are shared among the Federation, the provinces and the local levels in the ratio ______.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 50 : 25 : 25<\/li>\n<li>(b) 60 : 20 : 20<\/li>\n<li>(c) 70 : 15 : 15<\/li>\n<li>(d) 80 : 10 : 10<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — 70 : 15 : 15 under the Inter-Governmental Fiscal Arrangement Act, 2074. The ratio 50 : 25 : 25 is for royalty from natural resources.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.4.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the grant with its description.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fiscal equalisation grant — A. Matching money for infrastructure projects<\/li>\n<li>Conditional grant — B. Based on the need for spending and the capacity to raise revenue<\/li>\n<li>Complementary grant — C. Tied to a named programme or standard<\/li>\n<li>Special grant — D. For a specific purpose such as basic services or the uplift of disadvantaged groups<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1-D, 2-C, 3-A, 4-B<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Equalisation follows need and capacity; conditional is tied; complementary is matching money; special is for a specific purpose.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.4.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which body recommends the fiscal equalisation grants to be given to the provinces and local levels?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) The National Planning Commission<\/li>\n<li>(b) Nepal Rastra Bank<\/li>\n<li>(c) The Auditor General<\/li>\n<li>(d) The National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Articles 60 (3) and 251 (1)(b).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.4.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consider these statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Only the Government of Nepal may take foreign loans.<\/li>\n<li>Royalty from natural resources is shared 50 : 25 : 25.<\/li>\n<li>Local levels present their budget by 10 Ashad.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which of them are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — All three are correct (Article 59 (6) and the Inter-Governmental Fiscal Arrangement Act, 2074).<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h45\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> State the revenue jurisdiction of the three tiers of government and explain how revenue is shared among them.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> <strong>Jurisdiction.<\/strong> The <strong>Federation<\/strong> levies customs, excise, value-added tax, corporate and personal income tax, and passport and visa fees (Schedule 5). A <strong>province<\/strong> levies agro-income tax and, with the local level, house and land registration fee, vehicle tax, entertainment tax and advertisement tax (Schedule 6). A <strong>local level<\/strong> levies property tax, house rent tax, business tax and land tax, and shares the four taxes just named (Schedule 8). All three may charge service fees and receive royalty from natural resources (Schedule 9). <strong>Sharing.<\/strong> Because the largest taxes are federal, the Inter-Governmental Fiscal Arrangement Act, 2074 shares <strong>value-added tax and domestic excise 70 : 15 : 15<\/strong> among the Federation, provinces and local levels, and <strong>royalty from natural resources 50 : 25 : 25<\/strong>. Taxes common to provinces and local levels are collected by one level and shared 60 : 40. In addition the Federation gives four <strong>grants<\/strong> — equalisation, conditional, complementary and special. The <strong>National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission<\/strong> recommends the shares and the equalisation grants, so that the division is balanced and transparent, as Article 60 requires.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h46\">4.5 Elections and the political system (निर्वाचन र राजनीतिक प्रणाली)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h47\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>Bagmati's unit 1.8 names the &quot;political system&quot;. Election dates, the two voting systems, the 3 percent threshold and the newest results are standard questions in every province's current-affairs share.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h48\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>Nepal is a <strong>federal democratic republic<\/strong> with a <strong>multi-party, competitive<\/strong> system. The people are sovereign and use that power by voting. Every citizen aged <strong>18<\/strong> may vote. Governments at all three levels are formed through elections held every five years.<\/p>\n<p>Two voting systems are used. In <strong>first-past-the-post<\/strong> (पहिलो हुने निर्वाचित हुने) the candidate with the most votes in a constituency wins. In <strong>proportional representation<\/strong> (समानुपातिक) the voter chooses a party, and parties get seats in proportion to their votes. The House of Representatives and the Provincial Assemblies use <strong>both<\/strong> (a mixed system). Local elections use only first-past-the-post.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>Election Commission<\/strong> (निर्वाचन आयोग), a constitutional body, runs all elections. <strong>Political parties<\/strong> (राजनीतिक दल) must register with it.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h49\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>The system<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Form of government: <strong>parliamentary<\/strong>, with the Prime Minister answerable to the House of Representatives (Chapter 3).<\/li>\n<li>Article 56 (6) lists what all levels must protect: a multi-party, competitive, democratic, republican, federal system; human rights; rule of law; separation of powers and checks and balances; inclusive representation.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Voting age: 18.<\/strong> Minimum age of candidates: local level <strong>21<\/strong>; Provincial Assembly and House of Representatives <strong>25<\/strong>; National Assembly <strong>35<\/strong>; President <strong>45<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Election Commission — Articles 245 to 247<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>Chief Election Commissioner<\/strong> and <strong>4 Commissioners<\/strong>; appointed by the President on the recommendation of the Constitutional Council; term <strong>6 years<\/strong>; leave at age <strong>65<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>It conducts, supervises, directs and controls the elections of the <strong>President, Vice-President, members of the Federal Parliament, members of the Provincial Assemblies and members of the local levels<\/strong>, and prepares the voters' roll.<\/li>\n<li>It holds a <strong>referendum<\/strong> when the Federal Parliament decides on one by a two-thirds majority (Article 275).<\/li>\n<li>It registers political parties. All three levels of government must give it staff and help (Article 247).<\/li>\n<li>🔄 <strong>Chief Election Commissioner:<\/strong> <strong>Man Bahadur Karki<\/strong>, appointed on 14 Shrawan 2083 (30 July 2026). Before him, Ram Prasad Bhandari led the Commission as acting chief during the election of March 2026.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Who is elected how<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Body<\/th>\n<th>Seats<\/th>\n<th>System<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>House of Representatives<\/td>\n<td>275<\/td>\n<td>165 first-past-the-post + 110 proportional<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National Assembly<\/td>\n<td>59<\/td>\n<td>56 elected by provincial electoral colleges (8 from each province) + 3 nominated by the President<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Provincial Assemblies<\/td>\n<td>550<\/td>\n<td>330 first-past-the-post + 220 proportional<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local levels<\/td>\n<td>35,221 posts<\/td>\n<td>First-past-the-post only<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>President and Vice-President<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>Electoral college of members of the Federal Parliament and the Provincial Assemblies<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Thresholds and party status<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A party needs at least <strong>3 percent<\/strong> of the valid proportional votes to get proportional seats in the <strong>House of Representatives<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>For a <strong>Provincial Assembly<\/strong> the threshold is <strong>1.5 percent<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>National party<\/strong> (राष्ट्रिय दल): under section 52 of the <strong>Political Parties Act, 2073<\/strong>, a party needs <strong>3 percent<\/strong> of the proportional votes <strong>and at least one first-past-the-post seat<\/strong> in the House of Representatives.<\/li>\n<li>🔄 National parties after each election: <strong>5<\/strong> after 2074, <strong>7<\/strong> after 2079 and <strong>6<\/strong> after 2082 — Rastriya Swatantra Party, Nepali Congress, CPN (UML), Nepali Communist Party, Shram Sanskriti Party and Rastriya Prajatantra Party.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Political parties — Part 29, Articles 269 to 272<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>People who share a political idea may form a party; it must <strong>register with the Election Commission<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Its constitution and rules must be <strong>democratic<\/strong>; its office-bearers at federal and provincial levels must be elected <strong>at least once every five years<\/strong>; its committees must be inclusive.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 270:<\/strong> any law or decision that bans parties, or allows only one party or one ideology, is void.<\/li>\n<li>Women: at least <strong>one third<\/strong> of each party's members in the Federal Parliament and in each Provincial Assembly must be women.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Elections since 2064 BS<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Election<\/th>\n<th>Date<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First Constituent Assembly<\/td>\n<td>28 Chaitra 2064 (10 April 2008)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second Constituent Assembly<\/td>\n<td>4 Mangsir 2070 (19 November 2013)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local levels, three phases<\/td>\n<td>31 Baisakh, 14 Ashad and 2 Ashoj 2074 (14 May, 28 June and 18 September 2017)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>House of Representatives and Provincial Assemblies, two phases<\/td>\n<td>10 and 21 Mangsir 2074 (26 November and 7 December 2017)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local levels<\/td>\n<td>30 Baisakh 2079 (13 May 2022)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>House of Representatives and Provincial Assemblies<\/td>\n<td>4 Mangsir 2079 (20 November 2022)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>House of Representatives only<\/td>\n<td>21 Falgun 2082 (5 March 2026)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>The election of <strong>21 Falgun 2082<\/strong> was for the House of Representatives alone. The House had been dissolved on 27 Bhadra 2082 after the Gen Z movement; the Provincial Assemblies and local levels continued.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Result of 2082<\/strong> 🔄: Rastriya Swatantra Party <strong>182<\/strong> (125 + 57); Nepali Congress <strong>38<\/strong> (18 + 20); CPN (UML) <strong>25<\/strong> (9 + 16); Nepali Communist Party <strong>17<\/strong> (8 + 9); Shram Sanskriti Party <strong>7<\/strong> (3 + 4); Rastriya Prajatantra Party <strong>5<\/strong> (1 + 4); others <strong>1<\/strong>. Turnout was 59.67 percent.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Next elections<\/strong> 🔄: the local term ends on <strong>30 Baisakh 2084 (13 May 2027)<\/strong> and the Provincial Assembly term on <strong>4 Mangsir 2084 (19 November 2027)<\/strong>. In Baisakh 2083 the Election Commission proposed holding both on one date to save cost. Check whether dates have been fixed.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h50\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Two voting systems compared<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point<\/th>\n<th>First-past-the-post<\/th>\n<th>Proportional representation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepali name<\/td>\n<td>पहिलो हुने निर्वाचित हुने<\/td>\n<td>समानुपातिक<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voter chooses<\/td>\n<td>A candidate<\/td>\n<td>A party<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Area<\/td>\n<td>One constituency, one seat<\/td>\n<td>Whole country (House) or whole province (Assembly) as one constituency<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who wins<\/td>\n<td>The candidate with the most votes<\/td>\n<td>Parties, in proportion to votes, from a closed list<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Share of seats<\/td>\n<td>60 percent<\/td>\n<td>40 percent<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main strength<\/td>\n<td>Clear local representative<\/td>\n<td>Fair share for parties and inclusion of groups<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Threshold<\/td>\n<td>None<\/td>\n<td>3 percent (House); 1.5 percent (Provincial Assembly)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h51\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Articles on the Election Commission<\/td>\n<td>245 to 247<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Members of the Election Commission<\/td>\n<td>1 Chief + 4 Commissioners; 6 years<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voting age<\/td>\n<td>18<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seats in the House: first-past-the-post + proportional<\/td>\n<td>165 + 110<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seats in the Provincial Assemblies<\/td>\n<td>330 + 220<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Threshold for proportional seats<\/td>\n<td>3% (House); 1.5% (Provincial Assembly)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National party<\/td>\n<td>3% + one first-past-the-post seat<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National parties after 2082 🔄<\/td>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Part and Articles on parties<\/td>\n<td>Part 29; Articles 269 to 272<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Political Parties Act<\/td>\n<td>2073 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Last House election<\/td>\n<td>21 Falgun 2082 (5 March 2026)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Last provincial elections<\/td>\n<td>4 Mangsir 2079 (20 November 2022)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Last local elections<\/td>\n<td>30 Baisakh 2079 (13 May 2022)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h52\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;60 direct, 40 party&quot;<\/strong> — for the House (165 : 110) and for every Provincial Assembly.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Three and one&quot;<\/strong> makes a national party: 3 percent and 1 seat.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Local is only direct.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Election years &quot;74 – 79 – 82&quot;:<\/strong> all three levels in 2074; all three in 2079; the House alone in 2082.<\/li>\n<li><strong>National parties &quot;5 – 7 – 6.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h53\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The election of 2082 was for the <strong>House of Representatives only<\/strong>; the Provincial Assemblies were not re-elected.<\/li>\n<li>Local elections have <strong>no proportional seats<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The threshold is <strong>3 percent<\/strong> for the House but <strong>1.5 percent<\/strong> for a Provincial Assembly.<\/li>\n<li>Winning first-past-the-post seats alone does not make a national party; the <strong>3 percent<\/strong> is also needed.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Speaker<\/strong>, the <strong>Prime Minister<\/strong> and the <strong>Chief Minister<\/strong> are not elected through the Election Commission.<\/li>\n<li>The Election Commission has <strong>five<\/strong> members in all, not four.<\/li>\n<li><strong>National Assembly<\/strong> members are elected by an <strong>electoral college<\/strong>, not by the public.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>referendum<\/strong> needs a two-thirds decision of the Federal Parliament; the Election Commission only conducts it.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h54\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<p>Each province elects <strong>8<\/strong> members of the National Assembly. Its electoral college is made up of the members of the Provincial Assembly and the heads and deputy heads of the local levels in the province.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Assembly members<\/th>\n<th>Heads and deputies of local levels<\/th>\n<th>Electoral college<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>93<\/td>\n<td>274<\/td>\n<td>367<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>107<\/td>\n<td>272<\/td>\n<td>379<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>110<\/td>\n<td>238<\/td>\n<td>348<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>60<\/td>\n<td>170<\/td>\n<td>230<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>87<\/td>\n<td>218<\/td>\n<td>305<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>40<\/td>\n<td>158<\/td>\n<td>198<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>53<\/td>\n<td>176<\/td>\n<td>229<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Total: 550 + 1,506 = 2,056 voters. Their votes carry different weights fixed by federal law.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h55\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.5.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>How is the Election Commission of Nepal composed?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A Chief Election Commissioner and two Commissioners<\/li>\n<li>(b) A Chief Election Commissioner and four Commissioners<\/li>\n<li>(c) A Chief Election Commissioner and five Commissioners<\/li>\n<li>(d) A Chief Election Commissioner and six Commissioners<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Article 245 (1): five members in all.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.5.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>What share of the valid proportional votes must a party win to receive proportional seats in the House of Representatives?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 3 percent<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1.5 percent<\/li>\n<li>(c) 5 percent<\/li>\n<li>(d) 10 percent<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Three percent. The figure of 1.5 percent applies to Provincial Assemblies.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.5.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange these elections in order of time, earliest first.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>First phase of the first local elections under the present Constitution<\/li>\n<li>Election to the House of Representatives after the Gen Z movement<\/li>\n<li>Second local elections under the present Constitution<\/li>\n<li>First elections to the Provincial Assemblies<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 – 3 – 4 – 2<\/li>\n<li>(b) 4 – 1 – 3 – 2<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 – 4 – 2 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1 – 4 – 3 – 2<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Baisakh 2074, Mangsir 2074, Baisakh 2079, Falgun 2082.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.5.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consider these statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Local elections use only the first-past-the-post system.<\/li>\n<li>A national party needs 3 percent of the proportional votes and at least one first-past-the-post seat in the House of Representatives.<\/li>\n<li>All members of a Provincial Assembly are elected by proportional representation.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which of them are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Statement 3 is wrong: 60 percent of a Provincial Assembly is elected by first-past-the-post and 40 percent by proportional representation.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.5.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Three of these elections are conducted by the Election Commission. Which one is <strong>not<\/strong>?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Election of the Speaker of the House of Representatives<\/li>\n<li>(b) Election of the President<\/li>\n<li>(c) Election of members of a Provincial Assembly<\/li>\n<li>(d) Election of a ward chairperson<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — The House elects its own Speaker. Article 246 gives the Commission the elections of the President, Vice-President, Parliament, Provincial Assemblies and local levels.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h56\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> Describe the electoral systems used in Nepal for the three levels of government.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> Nepal uses a <strong>mixed electoral system<\/strong>. <strong>(1) House of Representatives:<\/strong> 275 members — 165 elected by <strong>first-past-the-post<\/strong>, one from each constituency, and 110 by <strong>proportional representation<\/strong>, with the whole country as one constituency and parties presenting closed, inclusive lists. A party needs 3 percent of the valid proportional votes to share those seats. <strong>(2) Provincial Assemblies:<\/strong> the same mix — 60 percent first-past-the-post (twice the province's House constituencies) and 40 percent proportional, with a 1.5 percent threshold. <strong>(3) Local levels:<\/strong> only first-past-the-post — voters directly elect the head, the deputy, the ward chairperson and four ward members. <strong>(4) Indirect elections:<\/strong> the National Assembly is elected by provincial electoral colleges of Assembly members and local heads, and the President by an electoral college of federal and provincial legislators. All citizens aged 18 may vote. The <strong>Election Commission<\/strong> conducts every one of these elections. The mixed system tries to give both a clear local representative and fair, inclusive representation; at least one third of each party's legislators must be women.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h57\">4.6 Civil service and the Public Service Commissions (निजामती सेवा र लोक सेवा आयोग)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h58\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>You are sitting an examination of a Province Public Service Commission to enter a provincial or local service. Questions on the Commission, on Article 244 and on the civil service are natural choices for examiners, and they return in the subjective papers.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h59\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>Ministers change; offices must keep working. The <strong>civil service<\/strong> (निजामती सेवा) is the body of permanent government staff who carry out law and policy. It is often called the &quot;permanent government&quot;. Staff are chosen on <strong>merit<\/strong> through open competition, not by the favour of leaders.<\/p>\n<p>To keep selection clean, the Constitution gives the job to an independent body, the <strong>Public Service Commission<\/strong> (लोक सेवा आयोग). Under federalism there are now eight such bodies: one federal Commission and a <strong>Province Public Service Commission<\/strong> (प्रदेश लोक सेवा आयोग) in each of the seven provinces. There are also three kinds of service: federal, provincial and local.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h60\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>In the Constitution<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Article 285:<\/strong> the Government of Nepal may form the <strong>federal civil service<\/strong> and other federal services under a <strong>federal Act<\/strong>. Posts are filled by <strong>competitive examination<\/strong> on the <strong>open and proportional inclusive<\/strong> principle. Provinces and local levels may form their own services <strong>by law<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 302:<\/strong> staff serving when the Constitution began could be <strong>adjusted<\/strong> to the federal, provincial and local levels. This was done under the <strong>Employee Adjustment Act, 2075<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Federal Public Service Commission — Articles 242 and 243:<\/strong>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>Chairperson and 4 members<\/strong>, appointed by the President on the recommendation of the Constitutional Council; term <strong>6 years<\/strong>; leave at age <strong>65<\/strong>;<\/li>\n<li>at least <strong>half<\/strong> of the members must have worked <strong>20 years or more<\/strong> in government service;<\/li>\n<li>it holds examinations to select candidates for the <strong>civil service<\/strong>;<\/li>\n<li>it holds the <strong>written examinations<\/strong> for the Nepal Army, Nepal Police, Armed Police Force, other federal services and public corporations in which the Government owns more than half;<\/li>\n<li>it must be consulted on the laws on conditions of service and on the principles of appointment, promotion and departmental action.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>Province Public Service Commission — Article 244:<\/strong> each province has one. Its work is fixed by <strong>provincial law<\/strong>; the grounds and standards are fixed by <strong>federal law<\/strong> — the <strong>Province Public Service Commission (Basis and Standards Determination) Act, 2075<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>A Province Public Service Commission selects staff for the <strong>provincial civil service<\/strong> and the <strong>local services<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>In Gandaki, for example, the provincial Act provides for a Chairperson and members recommended by a committee led by the Chief Minister, appointed by the Province Chief, for a term of six years. Each province has its own Act; read your own province's Act for details.<\/li>\n<li>The federal Commission was founded on <strong>1 Ashad 2008 (15 June 1951)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The present law<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The federal civil service still runs under the <strong>Civil Service Act, 2049<\/strong> and the Civil Service Rules, 2050. 🔄<\/li>\n<li><strong>Classes:<\/strong> gazetted (राजपत्राङ्कित) — special, first, second and third class; and non-gazetted (राजपत्र अनङ्कित) — first to fifth class.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ten services<\/strong> under the Act: Economic Planning and Statistics; Engineering; Agriculture; Judicial; Foreign; Administration; Audit; Forest; Miscellaneous; Education. The Health Service and the Parliament Service have Acts of their own.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Compulsory retirement age: 58<\/strong> under the present Act.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Inclusion:<\/strong> <strong>45 percent<\/strong> of posts filled by open competition are set aside. Taking that share as 100: women <strong>33<\/strong>, Adivasi Janajati <strong>27<\/strong>, Madhesi <strong>22<\/strong>, Dalit <strong>9<\/strong>, persons with disabilities <strong>5<\/strong>, backward areas <strong>4<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Heads of administration:<\/strong> the <strong>Chief Secretary<\/strong> (मुख्य सचिव) for the Federation; a <strong>Principal Secretary<\/strong> (प्रमुख सचिव) in each province; a <strong>Chief Administrative Officer<\/strong> (प्रमुख प्रशासकीय अधिकृत) in each local level.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Civil Service Day<\/strong> (निजामती सेवा दिवस) is <strong>22 Bhadra<\/strong>, recalling the first Civil Service Act of 2013 BS.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Federal Civil Service Bill<\/strong> 🔄<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Article 285 calls for a federal Act. A Bill was passed by the House of Representatives on <strong>15 Ashad 2082 (29 June 2025)<\/strong> and by the National Assembly on <strong>17 Bhadra 2082 (2 September 2025)<\/strong>, but it had not become law when the House was dissolved on 27 Bhadra 2082.<\/li>\n<li>A new <strong>Federal Civil Service Bill, 2083<\/strong> was registered in the House of Representatives on <strong>15 Ashoj 2083 (1 October 2026)<\/strong>. Reports call this the third time such a Bill has reached Parliament. <strong>It was still a Bill when this chapter was checked.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li>Reported proposals, which may change: a system of <strong>levels<\/strong> in place of classes; a retirement age of <strong>60<\/strong> with transitional rules; a <strong>two-year &quot;cooling-off&quot; period<\/strong> before retired senior officials may take other public appointments.<\/li>\n<li>Because the Act is missing, provinces and local levels have faced staff shortages. The Chief Ministers' Koshi Declaration of Ashoj 2083 asked for the Bill to be passed without delay.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The Commission today<\/strong> 🔄<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chairperson <strong>Madhav Prasad Regmi<\/strong> left office on <strong>3 Ashad 2083 (17 June 2026)<\/strong> on reaching the age limit of 65. Member <strong>Madhav Belbase<\/strong> became acting Chairperson. A new Chairperson was not confirmed when this chapter was checked.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h61\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Three levels of service<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point<\/th>\n<th>Federal<\/th>\n<th>Provincial<\/th>\n<th>Local<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Service<\/td>\n<td>Federal civil service<\/td>\n<td>Provincial civil service<\/td>\n<td>Local service<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>List<\/td>\n<td>Schedule 5, item 10<\/td>\n<td>Schedule 6, item 5<\/td>\n<td>Schedule 8, item 5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Law<\/td>\n<td>Federal Act (Article 285 (1))<\/td>\n<td>Provincial law (Article 285 (3))<\/td>\n<td>Law under Article 285 (3)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Selecting body<\/td>\n<td>Public Service Commission<\/td>\n<td>Province Public Service Commission<\/td>\n<td>Province Public Service Commission<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Head of staff<\/td>\n<td>Chief Secretary<\/td>\n<td>Principal Secretary<\/td>\n<td>Chief Administrative Officer<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Reserved share in open competition (45 percent taken as 100)<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group<\/th>\n<th>Share<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Women<\/td>\n<td>33<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adivasi Janajati<\/td>\n<td>27<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesi<\/td>\n<td>22<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dalit<\/td>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Persons with disabilities<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backward areas<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h62\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Articles on the Public Service Commission<\/td>\n<td>242 and 243<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Article on the Province Public Service Commission<\/td>\n<td>244<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Article on forming government services<\/td>\n<td>285<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Article on adjustment of staff<\/td>\n<td>302<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Members of the federal Commission<\/td>\n<td>Chairperson + 4; 6 years; age limit 65<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Federal Commission founded<\/td>\n<td>1 Ashad 2008 (15 June 1951)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public Service Commissions in Nepal<\/td>\n<td>8 (1 federal + 7 provincial)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Civil Service Act in force<\/td>\n<td>2049 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retirement age at present<\/td>\n<td>58<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reserved share<\/td>\n<td>45 percent<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Largest reserved share<\/td>\n<td>Women, 33<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Civil Service Day<\/td>\n<td>22 Bhadra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New Bill registered 🔄<\/td>\n<td>15 Ashoj 2083 (1 October 2026)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h63\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;242 body – 243 work – 244 province.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Reservation &quot;33 – 27 – 22 – 9 – 5 – 4&quot;<\/strong> adds up to 100; it is 45 percent of open posts.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Heads &quot;Chief – Principal – Chief Administrative&quot;:<\/strong> Federation, province, local level.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;285 builds the service, 302 moves the staff.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>2008 is the Commission's birth year<\/strong> — the year after democracy came in 2007.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h64\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The federal Commission has <strong>five<\/strong> members in all; &quot;four&quot; is only the members without the Chairperson.<\/li>\n<li>The Commission holds only the <strong>written<\/strong> examination for the army and police; it selects fully for the civil service.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>Province<\/strong> Public Service Commission is created by <strong>Article 244<\/strong>, but its powers come from <strong>provincial law<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Federal Civil Service Act<\/strong> did <strong>not<\/strong> exist when this chapter was checked. Do not write that it has been passed unless you have confirmed it.<\/li>\n<li><strong>45 percent<\/strong> is the reserved share; <strong>33<\/strong> is the women's part of that 45, not of all posts.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Chief Secretary<\/strong> is federal; <strong>Principal Secretary<\/strong> is provincial.<\/li>\n<li><strong>22 Bhadra<\/strong> is Civil Service Day; <strong>1 Ashad 2008<\/strong> is the Commission's founding date.<\/li>\n<li>The Commission <strong>recommends<\/strong>; the Government <strong>appoints<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h65\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Seat of the Province Public Service Commission<\/th>\n<th>Local levels whose staff it also selects<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Biratnagar<\/td>\n<td>137<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>Janakpurdham<\/td>\n<td>136<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Hetauda<\/td>\n<td>119<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>Pokhara<\/td>\n<td>85<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>Butwal<\/td>\n<td>109<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>Birendranagar<\/td>\n<td>79<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Dhangadhi<\/td>\n<td>88<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Part 2 of this book compares the 5th-level syllabi of these seven Commissions.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h66\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.6.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which Article of the Constitution provides for a Province Public Service Commission?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Article 242<\/li>\n<li>(b) Article 243<\/li>\n<li>(c) Article 244<\/li>\n<li>(d) Article 285<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Article 244. Articles 242 and 243 deal with the federal Commission; Article 285 with forming government services.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.6.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Of the posts set aside for inclusion in the civil service, what share goes to women?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 22 percent<\/li>\n<li>(b) 27 percent<\/li>\n<li>(c) 45 percent<\/li>\n<li>(d) 33 percent<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Thirty-three percent of the reserved share. Forty-five percent is the reserved share itself.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.6.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the head of administrative staff with the level.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chief Secretary — A. Local level<\/li>\n<li>Principal Secretary — B. Federation<\/li>\n<li>Chief Administrative Officer — C. Province<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1-A, 2-B, 3-C<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1-B, 2-C, 3-A<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1-C, 2-B, 3-A<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1-B, 2-A, 3-C<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Chief Secretary: Federation; Principal Secretary: province; Chief Administrative Officer: local level.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.6.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consider these statements about the federal Public Service Commission.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>It has a Chairperson and four members.<\/li>\n<li>The term of office is six years.<\/li>\n<li>It conducts the written examination for appointment to the Nepal Police.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which of them are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1 only<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — All three are correct (Articles 242 and 243).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.6.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Assertion (A): Every province of Nepal has its own Public Service Commission.\nReason (R): Article 244 provides for a Province Public Service Commission in each province, on standards fixed by federal law.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A.<\/li>\n<li>(b) Both A and R are true, and R explains A.<\/li>\n<li>(c) A is true, but R is false.<\/li>\n<li>(d) A is false, but R is true.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — The seven Commissions exist because Article 244 requires one in each province.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h67\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> What are the functions of the Public Service Commission? How is a Province Public Service Commission related to it?<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> The <strong>Public Service Commission<\/strong> is a constitutional body under Articles 242 and 243, with a Chairperson and four members appointed for six years. <strong>Functions:<\/strong> (1) it conducts examinations to select suitable candidates for posts in the civil service; (2) it conducts the written examinations for the Nepal Army, Nepal Police, Armed Police Force, other federal services and public corporations; (3) it is consulted on the laws about conditions of service and on the general principles of appointment, promotion and departmental action; (4) it reports each year to the President. Its purpose is fair selection on merit, free of political pressure, with inclusion. <strong>Province Public Service Commission:<\/strong> Article 244 gives every province its own Commission. It is not a branch of the federal body. Its functions are set by provincial law, but the grounds and standards are fixed by a federal Act of 2075 so that quality stays the same across the country. It selects staff for the provincial civil service and for the local levels of its province. Nepal therefore has eight Public Service Commissions.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h68\">4.7 Good governance, right to information and control of corruption (सुशासन, सूचनाको हक र भ्रष्टाचार नियन्त्रण)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h69\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>Citizen charter, information officer, 15 days, 35 days, the anti-corruption bodies and the yearly corruption index are short, checkable facts. They also give you ready points for essays in the subjective papers.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h70\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Good governance<\/strong> (सुशासन) means governing in a way that is lawful, open, fair, quick and answerable to the people. Its usual signs are: <strong>participation, rule of law, transparency, responsiveness, consensus, equity and inclusion, effectiveness and efficiency, and accountability<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Three tools support it.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right to information<\/strong> (सूचनाको हक): citizens may ask public bodies what they are doing.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Control of corruption<\/strong> (भ्रष्टाचार नियन्त्रण): bodies that prevent, investigate and punish the misuse of public office for private gain.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Service standards<\/strong>: a <strong>citizen charter<\/strong> (नागरिक बडापत्र), public hearings and complaint handling.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h71\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Good governance<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Article 51 (b)<\/strong> of the Constitution: public administration is to be fair, competent, impartial, transparent, <strong>free from corruption<\/strong>, accountable and participatory, so that people get services easily and equally.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Good Governance (Management and Operation) Act, 2064<\/strong> (सुशासन (व्यवस्थापन तथा सञ्चालन) ऐन, २०६४): authenticated on <strong>23 Magh 2064 (6 February 2008)<\/strong>; its Rules came in 2065. It requires:\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>citizen charter<\/strong> displayed in every office that serves the public;<\/li>\n<li><strong>public hearings<\/strong> (सार्वजनिक सुनुवाइ) by heads of offices at province, district and local level;<\/li>\n<li>a <strong>complaint box<\/strong>, opened <strong>every three days<\/strong>, and an officer responsible for complaints (nodal officer);<\/li>\n<li>a <strong>spokesperson<\/strong> (प्रवक्ता) in every ministry, department and office;<\/li>\n<li>use of <strong>information technology<\/strong> in office work.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li>A citizen charter tells the service-seeker <strong>which service, what documents, what fee, how long and which officer<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Provinces may make their own laws; Karnali, for example, has a Province Good Governance Act of 2076.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Right to information<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Article 27:<\/strong> every citizen has the right to ask for and receive information on any matter of personal or public concern, except information that the law keeps secret.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Right to Information Act, 2064<\/strong> (सूचनाको हक सम्बन्धी ऐन, २०६४), with Rules of 2065.<\/li>\n<li>Every public body appoints an <strong>Information Officer<\/strong> (सूचना अधिकारी).<\/li>\n<li>Information must be given <strong>at once<\/strong> if possible, otherwise <strong>within 15 days<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>If it is refused, the applicant complains to the <strong>head of the office within 7 days<\/strong>, and may then appeal to the <strong>National Information Commission within 35 days<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Public bodies must publish their main information on their own initiative <strong>every three months<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>National Information Commission<\/strong> (राष्ट्रिय सूचना आयोग): a <strong>Chief Information Commissioner and 2 Information Commissioners<\/strong>; term <strong>5 years<\/strong>; recommended by a committee chaired by the <strong>Speaker<\/strong>; formed on <strong>22 Baisakh 2065 (4 May 2008)<\/strong>. It is created by the Act, not by the Constitution.<\/li>\n<li><strong>National Information Day<\/strong> is <strong>3 Bhadra<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Control of corruption<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Commission for the Investigation of Abuse of Authority<\/strong> (अख्तियार दुरुपयोग अनुसन्धान आयोग), Articles 238 and 239: a Chief Commissioner and four Commissioners; it investigates corruption by holders of public office and files cases (Chapter 3).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Laws:<\/strong> Prevention of Corruption Act, 2059; Commission for the Investigation of Abuse of Authority Act, 2048; Special Court Act, 2059; Money Laundering Prevention Act, 2064; Public Procurement Act, 2063.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Special Court<\/strong> (विशेष अदालत): hears corruption cases; appeals go to the Supreme Court.<\/li>\n<li><strong>National Vigilance Centre<\/strong> (राष्ट्रिय सतर्कता केन्द्र): set up on <strong>27 Shrawan 2059 (12 August 2002)<\/strong> under section 37 of the Prevention of Corruption Act, under the direct supervision of the <strong>Prime Minister<\/strong>. Its work is <strong>preventive<\/strong>: awareness, monitoring and watching property statements.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Other watchdogs:<\/strong> the Auditor General; the Public Accounts Committee of the House; the Department of Money Laundering Investigation; the Department of Revenue Investigation; the Public Procurement Monitoring Office.<\/li>\n<li><strong>United Nations Convention against Corruption:<\/strong> adopted on 31 October 2003; Nepal is a party. <strong>International Anti-Corruption Day<\/strong> is <strong>9 December<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>🔄 <strong>Corruption Perceptions Index 2025<\/strong> (Transparency International, published 10 February 2026): Nepal scored <strong>34 out of 100<\/strong> and ranked <strong>109th of 182<\/strong> countries. The score was the same as the year before; the world average was 42.<\/li>\n<li>🔄 <strong>Property inquiry:<\/strong> on <strong>2 Baisakh 2083 (15 April 2026)<\/strong> the Government formed a commission led by former Supreme Court judge <strong>Rajendra Kumar Bhandari<\/strong> to collect and examine the property details of those who held high public office from 2062/63 to 2082/83.<\/li>\n<li>Anger at corruption and poor governance was a main cause of the Gen Z movement of Bhadra 2082 (Chapter 2).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h72\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Who does what against corruption<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Body<\/th>\n<th>Kind<\/th>\n<th>Main work<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Commission for the Investigation of Abuse of Authority<\/td>\n<td>Constitutional body<\/td>\n<td>Investigates and files corruption cases<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special Court<\/td>\n<td>Court<\/td>\n<td>Tries corruption cases<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National Vigilance Centre<\/td>\n<td>Under the Prime Minister<\/td>\n<td>Prevention and awareness<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auditor General<\/td>\n<td>Constitutional body<\/td>\n<td>Audits all government accounts<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public Accounts Committee<\/td>\n<td>Committee of the House<\/td>\n<td>Examines the Auditor General's report<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Department of Money Laundering Investigation<\/td>\n<td>Government department<\/td>\n<td>Investigates money laundering<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National Information Commission<\/td>\n<td>Body under an Act<\/td>\n<td>Protects the right to information<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Steps to get information<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step<\/th>\n<th>Time limit<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Apply to the Information Officer<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Information to be given<\/td>\n<td>At once, or within 15 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complaint to the head of the office<\/td>\n<td>Within 7 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Appeal to the National Information Commission<\/td>\n<td>Within 35 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h73\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Article on the right to information<\/td>\n<td>27<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Right to Information Act; Good Governance Act<\/td>\n<td>Both 2064 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good Governance Act authenticated<\/td>\n<td>23 Magh 2064 (6 February 2008)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Information to be given within<\/td>\n<td>15 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Appeal to the Commission within<\/td>\n<td>35 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Proactive disclosure<\/td>\n<td>Every 3 months<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National Information Commission<\/td>\n<td>1 Chief + 2 Commissioners; 5 years<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commission formed<\/td>\n<td>22 Baisakh 2065 (4 May 2008)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complaint box opened<\/td>\n<td>Every 3 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prevention of Corruption Act<\/td>\n<td>2059 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National Vigilance Centre set up<\/td>\n<td>27 Shrawan 2059 (12 August 2002)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>International Anti-Corruption Day<\/td>\n<td>9 December<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corruption Perceptions Index 2025 🔄<\/td>\n<td>Score 34; rank 109 of 182<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h74\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;64 – 64&quot;:<\/strong> the Good Governance Act and the Right to Information Act are both of 2064.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Ask – 15 – 7 – 35&quot;:<\/strong> ask the officer; 15 days; 7 days to the chief; 35 days to the Commission.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Information Commission &quot;1 + 2 for 5&quot;:<\/strong> three members, five years.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Vigilance prevents, the Commission investigates, the Special Court decides.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Signs of good governance &quot;P – R – T – R – C – E – E – A&quot;:<\/strong> Participation, Rule of law, Transparency, Responsiveness, Consensus, Equity, Effectiveness, Accountability.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h75\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>National Information Commission<\/strong> is <strong>not<\/strong> a constitutional body, and it has <strong>three<\/strong> members, not five.<\/li>\n<li>Its term is <strong>5 years<\/strong>; constitutional commissions serve <strong>6<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>National Vigilance Centre<\/strong> works under the <strong>Prime Minister<\/strong>; it does not file court cases.<\/li>\n<li>The right to information is <strong>Article 27<\/strong>; the right to communication is Article 19.<\/li>\n<li>Article 27 gives the right to <strong>citizens<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>higher<\/strong> score in the Corruption Perceptions Index means <strong>less<\/strong> corruption.<\/li>\n<li>The index measures <strong>perception<\/strong>, and its year (2025) differs from the year of publication (2026).<\/li>\n<li>The citizen charter comes from the <strong>Good Governance Act<\/strong>, not from the Right to Information Act.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h76\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<p>Every local level office and every ward office serves the public, so each must display a citizen charter and give information.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Local level offices<\/th>\n<th>Ward offices<\/th>\n<th>Service points close to the citizen<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>137<\/td>\n<td>1,157<\/td>\n<td>1,294<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>136<\/td>\n<td>1,271<\/td>\n<td>1,407<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>119<\/td>\n<td>1,121<\/td>\n<td>1,240<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>85<\/td>\n<td>759<\/td>\n<td>844<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>109<\/td>\n<td>983<\/td>\n<td>1,092<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>79<\/td>\n<td>718<\/td>\n<td>797<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>88<\/td>\n<td>734<\/td>\n<td>822<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Total: 7,496 offices (753 + 6,743).<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h77\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.7.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which Article of the Constitution guarantees the right to information?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Article 17<\/li>\n<li>(b) Article 19<\/li>\n<li>(c) Article 25<\/li>\n<li>(d) Article 27<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Article 27. Article 17 is the right to freedom, Article 19 the right to communication and Article 25 the right to property.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.7.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Under the Right to Information Act, 2064, if information cannot be given at once, within how many days must it be given?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 3 days<\/li>\n<li>(b) 7 days<\/li>\n<li>(c) 15 days<\/li>\n<li>(d) 35 days<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Fifteen days. Seven days is for the complaint to the head of the office, and 35 days for the appeal to the Commission.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.7.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the body with its work.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Commission for the Investigation of Abuse of Authority — A. Tries corruption cases<\/li>\n<li>Special Court — B. Preventive body under the Prime Minister's supervision<\/li>\n<li>National Vigilance Centre — C. Investigates corruption and files cases<\/li>\n<li>National Information Commission — D. Hears appeals when information is refused<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — The Commission investigates, the Special Court tries, the Vigilance Centre prevents and the Information Commission hears appeals.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.7.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consider these statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The Good Governance (Management and Operation) Act, 2064 requires offices serving the public to display a citizen charter.<\/li>\n<li>The National Information Commission has a Chief Information Commissioner and four Commissioners.<\/li>\n<li>Nepal scored 34 in the Corruption Perceptions Index 2025.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which of them are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Statement 2 is wrong: the Commission has a Chief Information Commissioner and two Commissioners.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.7.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Three of these are accepted signs of good governance. Which one is <strong>not<\/strong>?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Transparency<\/li>\n<li>(b) Accountability<\/li>\n<li>(c) Secrecy in all decisions<\/li>\n<li>(d) Rule of law<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Good governance calls for openness. Only information that the law protects may be kept secret.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h78\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> What legal and institutional arrangements has Nepal made to promote good governance?<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> <strong>Constitution:<\/strong> Article 51 (b) directs the State to make public administration fair, competent, transparent, free from corruption and accountable; Article 27 gives citizens the right to information. <strong>Laws:<\/strong> the Good Governance (Management and Operation) Act, 2064 requires a citizen charter, public hearings, complaint handling and a spokesperson in government offices. The Right to Information Act, 2064 requires every public body to appoint an Information Officer, give information within 15 days and publish key information every three months. The Prevention of Corruption Act, 2059 defines corruption offences. <strong>Institutions:<\/strong> the Commission for the Investigation of Abuse of Authority investigates and files cases; the Special Court tries them; the National Vigilance Centre works on prevention under the Prime Minister; the Auditor General and the Public Accounts Committee check public money; the National Information Commission hears appeals on information. <strong>Practice:<\/strong> electronic services, public audits and social audits reduce contact with middlemen. <strong>Weakness:<\/strong> Nepal's score of 34 in the Corruption Perceptions Index 2025 shows that laws alone are not enough; honest enforcement and citizen pressure are needed.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h79\">4.8 Electronic governance and public service delivery (विद्युतीय सुशासन र सार्वजनिक सेवा प्रवाह)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h80\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>Karnali's syllabus names electronic service delivery and e-governance. In every province, questions on the Nagarik App, the National Identity Card and &quot;Digital Nepal&quot; appear in the science and current-affairs share. This topic is also what the public will expect of you in office.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h81\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Public service delivery<\/strong> (सार्वजनिक सेवा प्रवाह) is the way the State hands its services to people: a citizenship certificate, a birth registration, a health check, a road, an allowance. Good delivery is <strong>easy to reach, quick, fair, low in cost and answerable<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Electronic governance<\/strong> or <strong>e-governance<\/strong> (विद्युतीय सुशासन) means using information and communication technology — computers, mobile phones and the internet — to give services, share information and run offices. The citizen does not have to stand in a queue or meet a middleman; the record stays in the system; the work can be traced. It is a tool of good governance, not a goal in itself.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h82\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Ideas<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Four kinds of e-governance link:<\/strong> Government to Citizen (<strong>G2C<\/strong>), Government to Business (<strong>G2B<\/strong>), Government to Government (<strong>G2G<\/strong>) and Government to Employee (<strong>G2E<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Stages of growth:<\/strong> giving information online → two-way interaction → full transactions online → joined-up services across offices.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Benefits:<\/strong> faster service; lower cost; service at any hour and from any place; transparency; fewer chances for corruption; better records.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Challenges:<\/strong> the <strong>digital divide<\/strong> between town and village; weak internet and electricity; low digital literacy; <strong>cyber security<\/strong> and data protection; systems of different offices that do not talk to each other; staff habits.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Laws and policies<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Electronic Transactions Act, 2063<\/strong> (विद्युतीय कारोबार ऐन, २०६३): gives legal recognition to <strong>electronic records and digital signatures<\/strong> and punishes computer crimes.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Good Governance (Management and Operation) Act, 2064:<\/strong> tells offices to use information technology in their work.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Information and Communication Technology Policy, 2072.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Digital Nepal Framework, 2076:<\/strong> approved by the Council of Ministers on <strong>5 Kartik 2076 (22 October 2019)<\/strong>; <strong>8 sectors<\/strong> and <strong>80 initiatives<\/strong>. The sectors are digital foundation, agriculture, health, education, energy, tourism, finance and urban infrastructure.<\/li>\n<li><strong>National Cyber Security Policy, 2080.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Institutions:<\/strong> 🔄 the <strong>Ministry of Information and Communication<\/strong> (called the Ministry of Communication and Information Technology until the ministries were regrouped in Baisakh 2083, May 2026); the Department of Information Technology, which has been under the <strong>Office of the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers<\/strong> since that regrouping; the National Information Technology Centre, which runs the Government Integrated Data Centre at Singha Durbar; the Office of the Controller of Certification (digital signatures); the Department of National ID and Civil Registration; the <strong>e-Governance Board<\/strong> at Singha Durbar.<\/li>\n<li>🔄 The e-Governance Board began under an order of 2079 BS as the Electronic Good Governance Commission. An amendment to the order in 2083 adds a government software development and operation centre.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Services in use<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nagarik App (नागरिक एप):<\/strong> one mobile application for many government services; launched in Magh 2077 (January 2021).<\/li>\n<li><strong>National Identity Card (राष्ट्रिय परिचयपत्र):<\/strong> a card with a unique number and biometric details. Distribution began on <strong>3 Mangsir 2075 (19 November 2018)<\/strong> in <strong>Panchthar<\/strong> district.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Hello Sarkar (हेल्लो सरकार):<\/strong> the complaint service at the Office of the Prime Minister; toll-free number <strong>1111<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Others:<\/strong> online application to the Public Service Commissions; electronic passport; online driving licence application; online tax filing and Permanent Account Number; the electronic procurement portal for public tenders; online company registration; online registration of personal events; electronic payment of government revenue.<\/li>\n<li>🔄 <strong>Policy and programme for 2083/84:<\/strong> at least <strong>100 government services<\/strong> through the Nagarik App; a database built on the rule &quot;give your details once, use them for every service&quot;; single service centres in all offices; essential services open seven days a week; the National Identity Card to be linked with banking, health, education and social security.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Public service delivery<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tools required by law:<\/strong> citizen charter; help desk; complaint box and nodal officer; public hearing; spokesperson and Information Officer (topic 4.7).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Other tools:<\/strong> token system; mobile service camps; one-door (single window) service; client satisfaction surveys; public and social audits.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ways of delivery:<\/strong> directly by government offices; through public–private partnership; by contract with private firms; through communities and cooperatives.<\/li>\n<li><strong>At the ward office:<\/strong> recommendations; registration of the five <strong>personal events<\/strong> — birth, death, marriage, divorce and migration; social security allowances.<\/li>\n<li>Under federalism most everyday services belong to the <strong>local level<\/strong> (Schedule 8), which is why the ward office matters most to citizens.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h83\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Four kinds of e-governance link<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Link<\/th>\n<th>Meaning<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>G2C<\/td>\n<td>Government to Citizen<\/td>\n<td>Applying for a driving licence online; Nagarik App<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>G2B<\/td>\n<td>Government to Business<\/td>\n<td>Bidding on the electronic procurement portal; online tax filing by a firm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>G2G<\/td>\n<td>Government to Government<\/td>\n<td>One office sharing records with another through the data centre<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>G2E<\/td>\n<td>Government to Employee<\/td>\n<td>Staff records, pay and leave handled online<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Traditional and electronic delivery compared<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point<\/th>\n<th>Traditional<\/th>\n<th>Electronic<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Place<\/td>\n<td>The office counter<\/td>\n<td>Anywhere with a phone or computer<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time<\/td>\n<td>Office hours<\/td>\n<td>Any hour<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Record<\/td>\n<td>Paper files<\/td>\n<td>Digital, searchable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contact with staff<\/td>\n<td>Face to face<\/td>\n<td>Little or none<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk<\/td>\n<td>Delay, middlemen, lost files<\/td>\n<td>Cyber attack, data leak, exclusion of those without access<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h84\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Electronic Transactions Act<\/td>\n<td>2063 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Information and Communication Technology Policy<\/td>\n<td>2072 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Digital Nepal Framework<\/td>\n<td>2076 BS; approved 5 Kartik 2076 (22 October 2019)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sectors and initiatives in the Framework<\/td>\n<td>8 and 80<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National Identity Card first distributed<\/td>\n<td>3 Mangsir 2075 (19 November 2018), Panchthar<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nagarik App launched<\/td>\n<td>Magh 2077 (January 2021)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hello Sarkar number<\/td>\n<td>1111<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kinds of e-governance link<\/td>\n<td>4 — G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personal events registered at the ward<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Services promised through the Nagarik App 🔄<\/td>\n<td>At least 100<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h85\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;8 and 80&quot;:<\/strong> Digital Nepal has 8 sectors and 80 initiatives.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Four G2s&quot;:<\/strong> Citizen, Business, Government, Employee.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;1111 — four ones to tell the Government.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Order of years &quot;63 – 72 – 75 – 76 – 77&quot;:<\/strong> Electronic Transactions Act, policy, identity card, Digital Nepal Framework, Nagarik App.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Good delivery &quot;E – Q – F – C – A&quot;:<\/strong> Easy, Quick, Fair, Cheap, Answerable.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h86\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>E-governance is <strong>not<\/strong> just a government website; it must change how the service is given.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Electronic Transactions Act<\/strong> is of <strong>2063<\/strong>; the <strong>Digital Nepal Framework<\/strong> is of <strong>2076<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The identity card began in <strong>Panchthar<\/strong>, not in Kathmandu.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Digital signature<\/strong> (legal, under the Act) is different from a scanned picture of a signature.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>digital divide<\/strong> is a challenge of e-governance, not a benefit.<\/li>\n<li><strong>G2B<\/strong> is government to <strong>business<\/strong>, not &quot;government to bank&quot;.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Hello Sarkar<\/strong> is at the <strong>Prime Minister's<\/strong> office, not at the President's.<\/li>\n<li>The Framework has <strong>80<\/strong> initiatives, not 8.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h87\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<p>Each provincial government runs its own offices and online services from its capital. Part 2 of this book records which syllabi name this subject.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Capital (seat of provincial services)<\/th>\n<th>How the syllabus brings in this topic<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Biratnagar<\/td>\n<td>Through science, technology and current affairs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>Janakpurdham<\/td>\n<td>Through science, technology and current affairs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Hetauda<\/td>\n<td>Governance is named in unit 1.8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>Pokhara<\/td>\n<td>Through science, technology and current affairs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>Deukhuri (Dang)<\/td>\n<td>Through science, technology and current affairs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>Birendranagar (Surkhet)<\/td>\n<td>Electronic service delivery, cyber security and e-governance are named<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Godawari (Kailali)<\/td>\n<td>Through science, technology and current affairs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>Chapter 7 (Science and Technology) returns to computers, the internet and cyber security.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h88\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.8.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which law gives legal recognition to electronic records and digital signatures in Nepal?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Electronic Transactions Act, 2063<\/li>\n<li>(b) Right to Information Act, 2064<\/li>\n<li>(c) Good Governance (Management and Operation) Act, 2064<\/li>\n<li>(d) Local Government Operation Act, 2074<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — The Electronic Transactions Act, 2063.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.8.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>How many sectors and initiatives does the Digital Nepal Framework, 2076 contain?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 5 sectors and 50 initiatives<\/li>\n<li>(b) 8 sectors and 80 initiatives<\/li>\n<li>(c) 10 sectors and 100 initiatives<\/li>\n<li>(d) 7 sectors and 77 initiatives<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Eight sectors and eighty initiatives.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.8.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the kind of e-governance link with the example.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>G2C — A. One ministry shares its records with another<\/li>\n<li>G2B — B. A citizen applies for a driving licence online<\/li>\n<li>G2G — C. A firm submits a bid through the electronic procurement portal<\/li>\n<li>G2E — D. An employee checks a pay record online<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Citizen, business, government and employee in that order.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.8.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consider these statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Distribution of the National Identity Card began in Panchthar district.<\/li>\n<li>The toll-free number of Hello Sarkar is 1111.<\/li>\n<li>The Nagarik App was launched before the Electronic Transactions Act was made.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which of them are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Statement 3 is wrong: the Act is of 2063 BS and the App was launched in 2077 BS.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 4.8.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Three of these are expected benefits of e-governance. Which one is <strong>not<\/strong>?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Faster service<\/li>\n<li>(b) More transparency<\/li>\n<li>(c) Lower cost of service<\/li>\n<li>(d) More dealings through middlemen<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — E-governance is meant to remove middlemen, not add them.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h89\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> What is e-governance? State its benefits and the main challenges it faces in Nepal.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> <strong>E-governance<\/strong> is the use of information and communication technology by the government to deliver services, share information, involve citizens and manage its own work. It links government with citizens (G2C), businesses (G2B), other government offices (G2G) and employees (G2E). <strong>Benefits:<\/strong> (1) services become faster and cheaper; (2) people can use them at any time and from any place; (3) work becomes transparent and traceable, so corruption and middlemen are reduced; (4) records are safe and easy to find; (5) offices can share data and plan better. <strong>Nepal's steps:<\/strong> the Electronic Transactions Act, 2063; the Digital Nepal Framework, 2076 with 8 sectors and 80 initiatives; the National Identity Card; the Nagarik App; online tax, procurement and Public Service Commission applications; Hello Sarkar. <strong>Challenges:<\/strong> the digital divide between towns and remote villages; weak internet and electricity; low digital literacy; cyber security and protection of personal data; systems of different offices that are not linked; and resistance to change. <strong>Way forward:<\/strong> reliable infrastructure, one shared database, strong data-protection law, trained staff and help desks for citizens who cannot use the technology.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h90\">Chapter 4 — End matter<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"h91\">A. Quick Revision<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Three tiers<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part 5, Articles 56–60. Three levels: Federation, Province, Local level. 761 governments (1 + 7 + 753).<\/li>\n<li>Schedules: 5 federal (35) · 6 provincial (21) · 7 federal and provincial (25) · 8 local (22) · 9 all three (15).<\/li>\n<li>Residual power: the Federation (Article 58). Jurisdiction disputes: Constitutional Bench (Article 137).<\/li>\n<li>Local level = rural municipalities, municipalities and District Assemblies. A district is not a level of government.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Province<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Province Chief: appointed by the President; 35 years; 5 years; not twice in one province.<\/li>\n<li>Chief Minister: Article 168; Council of Ministers at most 20 percent of the Assembly.<\/li>\n<li>Provincial Assembly: one house; 60 : 40; member 25 years; term 5 years; 550 seats in all.<\/li>\n<li>Days: Speaker 15; first session 20; confidence vote 30; whole process 35; assent 15; ordinance 60.<\/li>\n<li>Chief Attorney: Article 160. Capital and name: two thirds of the Assembly.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Local level<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>753 = 6 + 11 + 276 + 460; 6,743 wards; 35,221 directly elected posts.<\/li>\n<li>Village Executive adds 4 women + 2; Municipal Executive adds 5 women + 3.<\/li>\n<li>Ward Committee 1 + 4; Judicial Committee 3, led by the deputy; District Coordination Committee at most 9.<\/li>\n<li>Age 21; term 5 years; next election within six months.<\/li>\n<li>Local Government Operation Act, 2074: wards 5–21 and 9–35; sub-metropolitan 2 lakh; metropolitan 5 lakh; 13 disputes to decide + 11 to mediate.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Relations and money<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Article 232: cooperation, coexistence, coordination. Inter-Province Council: Article 234, chaired by the Prime Minister.<\/li>\n<li>Value-added tax and domestic excise 70 : 15 : 15. Royalty 50 : 25 : 25. Vehicle tax 60 province : 40 local.<\/li>\n<li>Four grants: equalisation, conditional, complementary, special.<\/li>\n<li>National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission: Articles 250–251; at most 5 members; 6 years.<\/li>\n<li>Budget days: 15 Jestha, 1 Ashad, 10 Ashad. Budget 2083/84: Rs 2,124.34 billion 🔄.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Elections<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Election Commission: Articles 245–247; 1 + 4; 6 years.<\/li>\n<li>House 165 + 110; Provincial Assemblies 330 + 220; local levels first-past-the-post only.<\/li>\n<li>Threshold 3 percent (House), 1.5 percent (Provincial Assembly). National party: 3 percent + 1 seat. Six national parties after 2082 🔄.<\/li>\n<li>Local elections 2074 (three phases) and 30 Baisakh 2079. House election 21 Falgun 2082 (5 March 2026).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Civil service<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Articles 242–243 federal Commission (1 + 4; founded 1 Ashad 2008); Article 244 Province Commission; Article 285 services; Article 302 adjustment.<\/li>\n<li>Civil Service Act, 2049 still in force; retirement at 58; reserved 45 percent — 33, 27, 22, 9, 5, 4.<\/li>\n<li>Federal Civil Service Bill, 2083 registered 15 Ashoj 2083; not yet an Act 🔄.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Good governance<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Good Governance Act, 2064: citizen charter, public hearing, complaint box, spokesperson.<\/li>\n<li>Right to information: Article 27; Act of 2064; 15 days; 7 days; 35 days; Commission of 1 + 2 for 5 years.<\/li>\n<li>Corruption: Commission for the Investigation of Abuse of Authority; Special Court; National Vigilance Centre (27 Shrawan 2059). Index 2025: score 34, rank 109 🔄.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>E-governance<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Electronic Transactions Act, 2063. Digital Nepal Framework, 2076: 8 sectors, 80 initiatives.<\/li>\n<li>National Identity Card: Panchthar, 3 Mangsir 2075. Nagarik App: Magh 2077. Hello Sarkar: 1111.<\/li>\n<li>Links: G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h92\">B. Top 20 facts<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Article 56<\/strong> makes Nepal a State of <strong>three levels<\/strong>: Federation, Province and Local level.<\/li>\n<li>Nepal has <strong>761 governments<\/strong>: 1 federal, 7 provincial and 753 local.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Schedules 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9<\/strong> hold <strong>35, 21, 25, 22 and 15<\/strong> items.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Residual power<\/strong> belongs to the <strong>Federation<\/strong> (Article 58).<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Province Chief<\/strong> is appointed by the <strong>President<\/strong>, must be <strong>35<\/strong>, and serves <strong>five years<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>A Provincial <strong>Council of Ministers<\/strong> may have at most <strong>20 percent<\/strong> of the Assembly's members.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>Provincial Assembly<\/strong> has <strong>one house<\/strong>; 60 percent of its seats are first-past-the-post and 40 percent proportional.<\/li>\n<li>The seven Provincial Assemblies have <strong>550 seats<\/strong>: Koshi 93, Madhesh 107, Bagmati 110, Gandaki 60, Lumbini 87, Karnali 40, Sudurpashchim 53.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>Village Executive<\/strong> adds <strong>4 women and 2<\/strong> Dalit or minority members; a <strong>Municipal Executive<\/strong> adds <strong>5 and 3<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Judicial Committee<\/strong> has <strong>3 members<\/strong> and is led by the <strong>Vice-Chairperson or Deputy Mayor<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>District Coordination Committee<\/strong> has <strong>at most 9 members<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Local Government Operation Act<\/strong> is of <strong>2074 BS<\/strong> (authenticated 29 Ashoj 2074).<\/li>\n<li>Relations among the levels rest on <strong>cooperation, coexistence and coordination<\/strong> (Article 232).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Value-added tax and domestic excise<\/strong> are shared <strong>70 : 15 : 15<\/strong>; <strong>royalty<\/strong> from natural resources <strong>50 : 25 : 25<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The four grants are <strong>equalisation, conditional, complementary and special<\/strong>; the <strong>National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission<\/strong> recommends the transfers.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Election Commission<\/strong> has a Chief Election Commissioner and <strong>4 Commissioners<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>A party needs <strong>3 percent<\/strong> of proportional votes for House seats, and <strong>3 percent plus one seat<\/strong> to be a national party.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 244<\/strong> gives each province a <strong>Province Public Service Commission<\/strong>; the federal Commission was founded on <strong>1 Ashad 2008<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Article 27<\/strong> gives the right to information; information must be given <strong>within 15 days<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Digital Nepal Framework, 2076<\/strong> has <strong>8 sectors and 80 initiatives<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h93\">C. Top 25 MCQs<\/h3>\n<p>None of these repeats a question from the topics.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which Part of the Constitution deals with the structure of the State and the distribution of State power?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Part 3<\/li>\n<li>(b) Part 4<\/li>\n<li>(c) Part 5<\/li>\n<li>(d) Part 20<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Part 5, Articles 56 to 60. Part 20 deals with relations among the three levels.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>How many items are there in the provincial list (Schedule 6)?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 21<\/li>\n<li>(b) 35<\/li>\n<li>(c) 25<\/li>\n<li>(d) 22<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Twenty-one. The other figures belong to Schedules 5, 7 and 8.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the subject with the Schedule in which it appears.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Town police — A. Schedule 5<\/li>\n<li>Provincial highways — B. Schedule 6<\/li>\n<li>Casino and lottery — C. Schedule 7<\/li>\n<li>Foreign and diplomatic affairs — D. Schedule 8<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1-D, 2-B, 3-C, 4-A<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Town police is local, provincial highways provincial, casino and lottery shared by the Federation and the Province, and foreign affairs federal.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>How many governments are there in Nepal when all three levels are counted?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 753<\/li>\n<li>(b) 761<\/li>\n<li>(c) 760<\/li>\n<li>(d) 77<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — One federal, seven provincial and 753 local governments.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consider these statements about the Province Chief.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The term of office is five years.<\/li>\n<li>A person cannot be Province Chief twice in the same province.<\/li>\n<li>The Province Chief is elected by the Provincial Assembly.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which of them are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — The Province Chief is appointed by the President, not elected (Article 163).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T6<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Within how many days must the Province Chief give assent to a Bill passed by the Provincial Assembly?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 15 days<\/li>\n<li>(b) 30 days<\/li>\n<li>(c) 35 days<\/li>\n<li>(d) 60 days<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Article 201 (2): fifteen days.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T7<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who appoints the Chief Attorney of a province?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) The President, on the recommendation of the Attorney General<\/li>\n<li>(b) The Province Chief, on the recommendation of the Chief Minister<\/li>\n<li>(c) The Attorney General<\/li>\n<li>(d) The Judicial Council<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Article 160 (2).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T8<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Fill in the blank. The number of first-past-the-post seats in a Provincial Assembly is ______ the number of House of Representatives constituencies in that province.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) equal to<\/li>\n<li>(b) one and a half times<\/li>\n<li>(c) three times<\/li>\n<li>(d) twice<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Article 176 (1)(a). Koshi, for example, has 28 House constituencies and 56 such seats.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T9<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which members does the Village Assembly add to a Village Executive?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Five women and three members from Dalit or minority communities<\/li>\n<li>(b) Three women and two members from Dalit or minority communities<\/li>\n<li>(c) Four women and two members from Dalit or minority communities<\/li>\n<li>(d) Two women and one member from Dalit or minority communities<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Article 215 (4). Five and three is the rule for a Municipal Executive.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T10<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In which year BS was the Local Government Operation Act made?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2074<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2055<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2072<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2077<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — It was authenticated on 29 Ashoj 2074.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T11<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who are the members of a District Assembly?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) All ward chairpersons of the district<\/li>\n<li>(b) Members of the Provincial Assembly elected from the district<\/li>\n<li>(c) The Chief District Officer and the heads of government offices<\/li>\n<li>(d) The Chairpersons and Vice-Chairpersons of the rural municipalities and the Mayors and Deputy Mayors of the municipalities in the district<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Article 220 (2).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T12<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consider these statements about Article 232.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Relations among the three levels rest on cooperation, coexistence and coordination.<\/li>\n<li>In certain grave situations the President may suspend or dissolve a Provincial Council of Ministers and Provincial Assembly for up to six months.<\/li>\n<li>Such a suspension or dissolution needs no approval from the Federal Parliament.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which of them are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — The action must be ratified by the Federal Parliament within 35 days; otherwise it lapses.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T13<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In what ratio is royalty from natural resources shared among the Federation, the province concerned and the local level concerned?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 70 : 15 : 15<\/li>\n<li>(b) 60 : 20 : 20<\/li>\n<li>(c) 50 : 25 : 25<\/li>\n<li>(d) 40 : 30 : 30<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — 50 : 25 : 25.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T14<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>What is the largest number of members, including the Chairperson, of the National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 5<\/li>\n<li>(b) 3<\/li>\n<li>(c) 7<\/li>\n<li>(d) 9<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Article 250 (1).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T15<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the level of government with the day by which its yearly budget is presented.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Federation — A. 10 Ashad<\/li>\n<li>Province — B. 15 Jestha<\/li>\n<li>Local level — C. 1 Ashad<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1-A, 2-B, 3-C<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1-B, 2-C, 3-A<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1-C, 2-B, 3-A<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1-B, 2-A, 3-C<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Federation 15 Jestha, province 1 Ashad, local level 10 Ashad.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T16<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In what ratio is vehicle tax shared between a province and its local levels?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 50 : 50<\/li>\n<li>(b) 40 : 60<\/li>\n<li>(c) 70 : 30<\/li>\n<li>(d) 60 : 40<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — The province collects it and keeps 60 percent; 40 percent goes to the local levels.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T17<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>On which date AD was the election to the House of Representatives of 21 Falgun 2082 held?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 20 November 2022<\/li>\n<li>(b) 13 May 2022<\/li>\n<li>(c) 5 March 2026<\/li>\n<li>(d) 27 March 2026<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — 5 March 2026. The other dates are the elections of 2079 and the swearing-in of the Prime Minister.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T18<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>How many parties gained the status of national party from the House of Representatives election of 2082 BS?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 6<\/li>\n<li>(b) 5<\/li>\n<li>(c) 7<\/li>\n<li>(d) 4<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Six. There were five after 2074 and seven after 2079.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T19<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Within what period after the end of the term of the local Assemblies must the next local election be held?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) One month<\/li>\n<li>(b) Three months<\/li>\n<li>(c) One year<\/li>\n<li>(d) Six months<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Article 225.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T20<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>When was the Public Service Commission of Nepal founded?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2004 BS<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 Ashad 2008 BS<\/li>\n<li>(c) 22 Bhadra 2013 BS<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2049 BS<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — 1 Ashad 2008 (15 June 1951).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T21<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange these groups by their share of the reserved posts in the civil service, largest first.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Dalit<\/li>\n<li>Women<\/li>\n<li>Madhesi<\/li>\n<li>Adivasi Janajati<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2 – 3 – 4 – 1<\/li>\n<li>(b) 4 – 2 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 – 4 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2 – 4 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Women 33, Adivasi Janajati 27, Madhesi 22, Dalit 9.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T22<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which Article requires a federal Act for forming and running the federal civil service?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Article 285<\/li>\n<li>(b) Article 244<\/li>\n<li>(c) Article 302<\/li>\n<li>(d) Article 232<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Article 285 (1).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T23<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who chairs the committee that recommends the Chief Information Commissioner?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) The Prime Minister<\/li>\n<li>(b) The Speaker of the House of Representatives<\/li>\n<li>(c) The Chief Justice<\/li>\n<li>(d) The Minister for Communication<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — The Speaker chairs it; the Minister and the head of the Federation of Nepali Journalists are members.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T24<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Assertion (A): A citizen charter supports good governance.\nReason (R): It tells the service-seeker the service, the time, the fee and the responsible officer, so that the office can be held to account.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A is true, but R is false.<\/li>\n<li>(b) A is false, but R is true.<\/li>\n<li>(c) Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A.<\/li>\n<li>(d) Both A and R are true, and R explains A.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — The charter makes the standard public, which is the basis of accountability.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T25<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The &quot;Koshi Declaration, 2083&quot; was issued by which gathering?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A meeting of the seven Province Chiefs<\/li>\n<li>(b) The federal Council of Ministers<\/li>\n<li>(c) The Inter-Province Chief Ministers' Conference at Biratnagar<\/li>\n<li>(d) The Election Commission<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — It was issued in Ashoj 2083 and dealt with fiscal federalism, the Federal Civil Service Bill and provincial police. 🔄<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h94\">D. Five short-answer questions<\/h3>\n<p><strong>1. Distinguish between exclusive and concurrent powers under the Constitution, with examples.<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> Article 57 · exclusive = one level alone: Schedule 5 (federal, 35 — defence, foreign affairs, currency), Schedule 6 (provincial, 21 — provincial police, provincial highways), Schedule 8 (local, 22 — basic and secondary education, local roads) · concurrent = shared: Schedule 7 (Federation and Province, 25 — criminal procedure, casino, tourism), Schedule 9 (all three, 15 — cooperatives, agriculture, disaster management) · lower-level law must not conflict with higher-level law (Article 57 (6)–(7)) · residual power with the Federation (Article 58) · disputes to the Constitutional Bench.<\/p>\n<p><strong>2. Write about the appointment, term and role of the Province Chief.<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> Articles 163–167 · representative of the Government of Nepal · appointed by the President; 35 years; qualified for the Federal Parliament · five years unless removed earlier; not twice in the same province · oath before the President · appoints the Chief Minister and ministers; summons and addresses the Assembly; assents to Bills; issues ordinances · acts on the recommendation of the Provincial Council of Ministers · exercises executive power during federal rule (Article 162 proviso) · ceremonial post.<\/p>\n<p><strong>3. What is a District Coordination Committee? State its formation and functions.<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> Article 220 · District Assembly = heads and deputies of all local levels in the district · it elects the Committee: at most 9 members — Chief, Deputy Chief, at least 3 women, at least 1 Dalit or minority member · term 5 years · functions: coordinate the local levels; monitor development work for balance; coordinate between federal and provincial offices and the local levels; other work under provincial law · helps the Provincial Assembly settle disputes (Article 235) · it coordinates, it does not govern; 77 in all.<\/p>\n<p><strong>4. Describe the composition and functions of the National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission.<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> Articles 250–251 · at most 5 members including the Chairperson; appointed by the President on the recommendation of the Constitutional Council; 6 years; age limit 65; minimum age 45; 20 years' experience · functions: basis for sharing revenue from the Federal Consolidated Fund; recommends equalisation grants; studies the basis of conditional grants; sharing between province and local levels; ceiling for internal loans; sharing of investment in and returns from natural resources; studies likely disputes · importance: balanced and transparent fiscal federalism (Article 60).<\/p>\n<p><strong>5. How can a citizen obtain information from a public office? Explain the procedure.<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> Article 27; Right to Information Act, 2064 · apply to the Information Officer · information at once or within 15 days · if refused, complain to the head of the office within 7 days · appeal to the National Information Commission within 35 days · the Commission may order the information to be given · some information may lawfully be kept secret (security, investigation, privacy and the like) · offices must publish key information every three months · links with the citizen charter and public hearing.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h95\">E. Common mistakes<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Calling the <strong>district<\/strong> a tier of government. The tiers are Federation, Province and Local level.<\/li>\n<li>Giving <strong>residual power<\/strong> to the provinces. It belongs to the Federation.<\/li>\n<li>Mixing <strong>Schedule 7<\/strong> (two levels) with <strong>Schedule 9<\/strong> (three levels).<\/li>\n<li>Writing that the Province Chief is <strong>elected<\/strong>. The President appoints.<\/li>\n<li>Writing <strong>25 ministers<\/strong> for a province. The limit is 20 percent of the Assembly.<\/li>\n<li>Swapping <strong>4 + 2<\/strong> (village) and <strong>5 + 3<\/strong> (municipality).<\/li>\n<li>Making the <strong>Mayor<\/strong> the head of the Judicial Committee. It is the deputy.<\/li>\n<li>Writing <strong>744<\/strong> local levels. There are 753.<\/li>\n<li>Applying <strong>70 : 15 : 15<\/strong> to all taxes. It applies to value-added tax and domestic excise only.<\/li>\n<li>Saying the Fiscal Commission <strong>distributes<\/strong> grants. It recommends; the Government distributes.<\/li>\n<li>Saying the Provincial Assemblies were re-elected in 2082. Only the House of Representatives was.<\/li>\n<li>Using the <strong>3 percent<\/strong> threshold for Provincial Assemblies. There it is 1.5 percent.<\/li>\n<li>Writing that the <strong>Federal Civil Service Act<\/strong> has been passed, without checking.<\/li>\n<li>Counting the <strong>National Information Commission<\/strong> as a constitutional body.<\/li>\n<li>Reading a <strong>higher<\/strong> corruption-index score as more corruption.<\/li>\n<li>Treating a <strong>government website<\/strong> as the whole of e-governance.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h96\">F. Status of the facts in this chapter<\/h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group of facts<\/th>\n<th>Status<\/th>\n<th>Basis<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Articles 56–60, 160–237, 240–251, 269–272, 275, 285, 288, 294, 295 and 302; Schedules 5 to 9 and their item counts<\/td>\n<td><strong>Checked against a text of the Constitution<\/strong> during this research<\/td>\n<td>The English translation was read Article by Article; the numbers in about thirty Articles were compared with the Nepali text. Both are unofficial copies<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Article 232 (4) — the majority needed to ratify a suspension<\/td>\n<td>⚖️ The Nepali text is followed<\/td>\n<td>Nepali text: majority of all existing members. English copy: two thirds<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local Government Operation Act, 2074: date, ward limits, classification of cities, meetings, 13 + 11 disputes<\/td>\n<td>Checked through extracts<\/td>\n<td>Extracts of the Law Commission's page shown in search results, and secondary articles. The full Act was not read<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inter-Governmental Fiscal Arrangement Act, 2074: 70 : 15 : 15, 50 : 25 : 25, 60 : 40, budget days<\/td>\n<td>Consistent across secondary sources<\/td>\n<td>Articles that cite sections 5, 6, 7 and 21. The Act itself was not read<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coordination and Inter-relation Act, 2077: the councils<\/td>\n<td>Checked against news reports<\/td>\n<td>The Act itself was not read<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good Governance Act, 2064; Right to Information Act, 2064; National Information Commission; National Vigilance Centre<\/td>\n<td>Checked through extracts<\/td>\n<td>Law Commission extract; the Commission's and the Centre's web pages as shown in search results<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Civil Service Act, 2049: classes, ten services, retirement age 58; separate Acts for the Health Service and the Parliament Service; Employee Adjustment Act, 2075; Province Public Service Commission (Basis and Standards Determination) Act, 2075; what each of the four grants is for; Civil Service Day; signs of good governance; convention against corruption; National Information Day; Special Court; Hello Sarkar; Nagarik App launch month; institutions and policies of information technology; sectors of the Digital Nepal Framework<\/td>\n<td><strong>Standard reference facts<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Not re-opened in an official source this time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reserved shares in the civil service; thresholds of 3 and 1.5 percent; rule for a national party<\/td>\n<td>Checked against news reports<\/td>\n<td>Reports citing the Acts<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chief Ministers and Province Chiefs<\/td>\n<td>Checked during this research 🔄<\/td>\n<td>News reports (July–November 2026) and encyclopaedia pages as they stood in September–October 2026. Not checked on the provinces' own websites<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chief Election Commissioner; Chairperson of the Public Service Commission<\/td>\n<td>Checked during this research 🔄<\/td>\n<td>News reports of June and July 2026<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Budget, grants and loan ceiling for 2083/84<\/td>\n<td>Checked during this research 🔄<\/td>\n<td>News reports of the budget speech and of the Ministry of Finance's statement; small differences between the two are noted<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Names of the ministries that handle communication and information technology after the regrouping of Baisakh 2083 (18 ministries in place of 22)<\/td>\n<td><strong>Added on 11 November 2026<\/strong>, during the research for Chapter 7 🔄<\/td>\n<td>News reports of the Council of Ministers' decision of May 2026<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Federal Civil Service Bill<\/td>\n<td>Checked during this research 🔄<\/td>\n<td>News reports to October 2026; later progress could not be ruled out<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Election results of 2082; number of national parties<\/td>\n<td>Carried from Chapter 3 and news reports 🔄<\/td>\n<td>Not checked on the Election Commission's website<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corruption Perceptions Index 2025; property inquiry commission; Koshi Declaration<\/td>\n<td>Checked against news reports 🔄<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wards in each province<\/td>\n<td>Three figures seen in sources; four from standard reference lists<\/td>\n<td>The seven figures add up to 6,743<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Majorities, two-thirds figures, 20 percent limits, electoral colleges, 35,221 posts<\/td>\n<td>Worked out by arithmetic<\/td>\n<td>From the Articles and the counts in Chapters 1 and 3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All BS–AD date pairs<\/td>\n<td>Checked with a calendar-conversion tool<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Facts to re-check under UPDATE (🔄):<\/strong> Chief Ministers and Province Chiefs, especially the new Province Chief of Koshi · dates of the local and Provincial Assembly elections due in 2084 · whether the Federal Civil Service Bill has become an Act · the new Chairperson of the Public Service Commission · the next budget and grant figures · any change in the number or classification of local levels · the next Corruption Perceptions Index · progress on the Nagarik App and the e-Governance Board · any constitutional amendment touching the provinces.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h97\">G. References<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Official bodies to consult for the final word<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nepal Law Commission — the Constitution and the Acts named in this chapter<\/li>\n<li>Election Commission, Nepal — election dates, results and party status<\/li>\n<li>Ministry of Finance — the budget speech and the statement of fiscal transfers<\/li>\n<li>National Natural Resources and Fiscal Commission — recommendations on grants, revenue sharing and loan ceilings<\/li>\n<li>Public Service Commission and your Province Public Service Commission — notices, syllabus and Acts<\/li>\n<li>Office of the Province Chief and Office of the Chief Minister of your province<\/li>\n<li>National Information Commission; National Vigilance Centre; Commission for the Investigation of Abuse of Authority<\/li>\n<li>Ministry of Information and Communication; Office of the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers (information technology); e-Governance Board<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Sources used for this chapter<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Text of the Constitution (unofficial copies): <a href=\"https://github.com/computerclubkec/constitution-of-nepal-dataset\">https://github.com/computerclubkec/constitution-of-nepal-dataset<\/a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/nischhal-hub/constitution-of-nepal-np-eng-dataset\">https://github.com/nischhal-hub/constitution-of-nepal-np-eng-dataset<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Chief Ministers: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_minister_(Nepal)\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_minister_(Nepal)<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/08/2000389/kiran-thapa-appointed-as-chief-minister-of-bagmati\">https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/08/2000389/kiran-thapa-appointed-as-chief-minister-of-bagmati<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.ratopati.com/story/580446/mangal-bahadur-shahi-is-being-appointed-as-the-5th-chief-minister-of-karnali-today\">https://www.ratopati.com/story/580446/mangal-bahadur-shahi-is-being-appointed-as-the-5th-chief-minister-of-karnali-today<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.annapurnapost.com/story/504193/\">https://www.annapurnapost.com/story/504193/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://arthakoartha.com/archives/115141\">https://arthakoartha.com/archives/115141<\/a> and <a href=\"https://kharibot.com/news-details/9985/kharibot\">https://kharibot.com/news-details/9985/kharibot<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Province Chiefs: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor_(Nepal)\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor_(Nepal)<\/a>, <a href=\"https://radionepalonline.com/2026/11/01/745157.html\">https://radionepalonline.com/2026/11/01/745157.html<\/a> and <a href=\"https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/11/2049000/parashuram-who-remained-unchanged-despite-changes-in-power-and-equations-is-the-first-provincial-chief-to-complete-a-5-year-term\">https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/11/2049000/parashuram-who-remained-unchanged-despite-changes-in-power-and-equations-is-the-first-provincial-chief-to-complete-a-5-year-term<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Koshi Declaration: <a href=\"https://www.gorkhapatraonline.com/news/226746\">https://www.gorkhapatraonline.com/news/226746<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Local levels: <a href=\"https://bihanionline.com/archives/7955\">https://bihanionline.com/archives/7955<\/a>, <a href=\"https://ejanakpurtoday.com/2023/03/19/73211/\">https://ejanakpurtoday.com/2023/03/19/73211/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.ronbupdate.com/2021/01/pradesh-no-1.html\">https://www.ronbupdate.com/2021/01/pradesh-no-1.html<\/a> and <a href=\"https://sudurkhabar.com/archives/383333\">https://sudurkhabar.com/archives/383333<\/a>; the Law Commission's repository page for the Local Government Operation Act, 2074, as shown in search results<\/li>\n<li>Revenue sharing and budget days: <a href=\"https://prasashan.com/2024/01/03/507881/\">https://prasashan.com/2024/01/03/507881/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.prasashan.com/2020/08/17/191536/\">https://www.prasashan.com/2020/08/17/191536/<\/a> and <a href=\"https://prasashan.com/2020/05/29/180552/\">https://prasashan.com/2020/05/29/180552/<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Budget and grants for 2083/84: <a href=\"https://rajdhanidaily.com/id/138523/\">https://rajdhanidaily.com/id/138523/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/05/1942523/the-budget-going-to-the-lower-government-next-year-is-around-rs-6-trillion\">https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/05/1942523/the-budget-going-to-the-lower-government-next-year-is-around-rs-6-trillion<\/a>, <a href=\"https://nepsetrading.com/news/federal-fiscal-transfers-for-fy-208384-who-gets-what\">https://nepsetrading.com/news/federal-fiscal-transfers-for-fy-208384-who-gets-what<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.gorkhapatraonline.com/news/209477\">https://www.gorkhapatraonline.com/news/209477<\/a> and <a href=\"https://arthasarokar.com/2026/03/domestic-debt-limit-set-for-next-fiscal-year.html\">https://arthasarokar.com/2026/03/domestic-debt-limit-set-for-next-fiscal-year.html<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Coordination among the levels: <a href=\"https://www.karobardaily.com/news/247226\">https://www.karobardaily.com/news/247226<\/a> and <a href=\"https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/11/2049995/parliamentary-committee-examining-the-status-of-federalism-implementation\">https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/11/2049995/parliamentary-committee-examining-the-status-of-federalism-implementation<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Elections and parties: <a href=\"https://www.ratopati.com/story/562817/commission-proposes-to-hold-local-and-provincial-assembly-elections-simultaneously-claims-to-save-rs-4-billion\">https://www.ratopati.com/story/562817/commission-proposes-to-hold-local-and-provincial-assembly-elections-simultaneously-claims-to-save-rs-4-billion<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2025/10/1775222/the-election-schedule-has-been-changed-local-and-provincial-elections-will-be-held-after-the-union-elections\">https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2025/10/1775222/the-election-schedule-has-been-changed-local-and-provincial-elections-will-be-held-after-the-union-elections<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.bbc.com/nepali/articles/clygg5zd6xyo\">https://www.bbc.com/nepali/articles/clygg5zd6xyo<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.daksumedia.com/parliament/2026-03-40084\">https://www.daksumedia.com/parliament/2026-03-40084<\/a>, <a href=\"https://nepalnews.com/2026/01/30/how-many-of-the-58-will-be-national-parties-this-time/\">https://nepalnews.com/2026/01/30/how-many-of-the-58-will-be-national-parties-this-time/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.nepalilink.com/2022/12/14/64307.html\">https://www.nepalilink.com/2022/12/14/64307.html<\/a>, <a href=\"https://himalayapost.com/archives/23697\">https://himalayapost.com/archives/23697<\/a> and <a href=\"https://himalpress.com/2026/07/newly-appointed-chief-election/\">https://himalpress.com/2026/07/newly-appointed-chief-election/<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Civil service: <a href=\"https://www.nepaljapan.com/10/02/740143/\">https://www.nepaljapan.com/10/02/740143/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.ratopati.com/story/498008/federal-civil-service-bill-passed-by-the-house-of-representatives\">https://www.ratopati.com/story/498008/federal-civil-service-bill-passed-by-the-house-of-representatives<\/a>, <a href=\"https://ktmdainik.com/2025/09/13510/\">https://ktmdainik.com/2025/09/13510/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.kanunkhabar.com/contemporary/33457/\">https://www.kanunkhabar.com/contemporary/33457/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/06/1956291/public-service-commission-chairman-regmi-resigns-due-to-age-limit\">https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2026/06/1956291/public-service-commission-chairman-regmi-resigns-due-to-age-limit<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2024/12/1585293/25000-employees-in-civil-service-through-reservation-quota-in-17-years\">https://www.onlinekhabar.com/2024/12/1585293/25000-employees-in-civil-service-through-reservation-quota-in-17-years<\/a> and <a href=\"https://annapurnapost.com/story/176858/\">https://annapurnapost.com/story/176858/<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Good governance, information and corruption: <a href=\"https://nic.gov.np/page/about-us\">https://nic.gov.np/page/about-us<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.tukhabar.com/2020/10/99164/\">https://www.tukhabar.com/2020/10/99164/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.nvc.gov.np/pages/introduction-1/\">https://www.nvc.gov.np/pages/introduction-1/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://risingnepaldaily.com/news/75445\">https://risingnepaldaily.com/news/75445<\/a>, <a href=\"https://ekantipur.com/news/2026/04/15/commission-formed-to-investigate-assets-of-high-ranking-officials-since-2062-51-56.html\">https://ekantipur.com/news/2026/04/15/commission-formed-to-investigate-assets-of-high-ranking-officials-since-2062-51-56.html<\/a> and <a href=\"https://www.yugnews.com/2023/20/73346/\">https://www.yugnews.com/2023/20/73346/<\/a>; the Law Commission's repository page for the Good Governance (Management and Operation) Act, 2064, as shown in search results<\/li>\n<li>E-governance: <a href=\"https://www.sailungonline.com/12601/24/10/2019/\">https://www.sailungonline.com/12601/24/10/2019/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://kantipurtv.com/news/2023/02/10/1676009883.html\">https://kantipurtv.com/news/2023/02/10/1676009883.html<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.annapurnapost.com/story/499665/\">https://www.annapurnapost.com/story/499665/<\/a>, <a href=\"https://www.gorkhapatraonline.com/news/216007\">https://www.gorkhapatraonline.com/news/216007<\/a> and <a href=\"https://egc.gov.np/content/2477/electronic-good-government-commission--formation-and-operations-/\">https://egc.gov.np/content/2477/electronic-good-government-commission--formation-and-operations-/<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The news sites, the encyclopaedia pages and the study websites are not official sources. They were used because the official sites could not be opened directly, and they were compared with each other.<\/p>\n<p><em>End of Chapter 4 (Part 8).<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> Part 9 — Chapter 5: Economy and Public Finance. Type <strong>CONTINUE<\/strong>.<\/p>\n"});
